/* Quiet Time — the psyc paper: the bundled discovery collection. Content only; rendering lives in psyc.js.

   level:
     checked  — the claims below were checked against the article's methods and results (full text), or come
                from a digest in psyc-issues.js that was read in full.
     abstract — written from the authors' abstract only; the full text was not checked. Claims are attributed.
   Never promote an item to "checked" without reading the article.

   links: [targetId, relation, why]. Relations:
     cites / citedby          — the reference list really contains the paper (validated against
                                data/psyc-citation-graph.json by scripts/psyc-check.mjs)
     failed / failedby        — a replication attempt that did not reproduce the original
     replicates / replicatedby— a replication that did
     challenges / challengedby— a direct disagreement in print
     idea                     — the same theoretical idea or mechanism, no claim of influence
     cousin                   — thematic similarity only; says so to the reader
   Inline citations use double square brackets around a PSYC_BIB key, a pipe and a locator. */
const PSYC_DISCOVERIES = [
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ cognitive
{ id: 'radiologist-gorilla', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'checked', bib: 'drew2013', design: 'lab experiment with eye tracking',
  head: 'radiologists scrolled past a gorilla in a lung scan, and most of them looked right at it',
  card: 'Trafton Drew and colleagues hid a gorilla, 48 times the size of an average nodule, in the final CT case given to 24 radiologists searching for lung nodules. Twenty of them never mentioned it. Eye tracking showed that 12 of those 20 had looked directly at where it was. Expertise made them far better at finding nodules. It did not switch off the limits of attention.',
  story: [
    'Inattentional blindness, missing something obvious while busy with something else, is usually shown with novices doing an unfamiliar task like counting basketball passes. So the sharper question is what happens to people who have spent years training their eyes on exactly this kind of image [[drew2013|introduction]].',
    'Twenty-four radiologists had up to three minutes each to scroll through five lung CTs looking for nodules, while an eye tracker followed their gaze. In the last case, a gorilla the size of a matchbook sat on a slice that also contained a nodule. Twenty of the 24 did not report it. Of those, 12 had fixated its location [[drew2013|Exp. 1, Results]]. That is the unsettling part: the eyes were on it. Looking and noticing turn out to be different events.',
    'A fair worry is that the gorilla was simply hard to see. So a control experiment told observers to look for it, and they were near ceiling (88% correct) even when it was on screen for only a fraction of a second [[drew2013|Exp. 3]].',
    'Experts did do somewhat better than 25 untrained observers on noticing the gorilla, though only just (<em>p</em> = .0497), and enormously better on the actual job: 55% versus 12% of nodules found [[drew2013|Exp. 2]]. The authors’ reading is not that radiologists are careless. It is that even deep expertise does not immunise anyone against the architecture of attention [[drew2013|Discussion]].'
  ],
  how: ['24 radiologists (mean age 48) in Experiment 1; 25 observers with no medical training in Experiment 2; a separate detection experiment with the gorilla present on half of 20 trials in Experiment 3 [[drew2013|Design and Procedure]].', 'A single, unexpected test case for each person: this is a striking demonstration rather than an estimate of how often real findings are missed in clinical practice.'],
  links: [['invisible-gorilla-original', 'cites', 'The study that made gorillas a unit of measurement in attention research, and which Drew and colleagues build on.'], ['russian-blues', 'cousin', 'Another way that what you see depends on more than what lands on your retina: here attention, there language.'], ['attention-and-inflammation', 'cousin', 'If attention can decide what you notice, can it change what your body does? A recent and much stranger question.']] },

{ id: 'invisible-gorilla-original', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'abstract', bib: 'simons1999', design: 'lab experiment', classic: true,
  head: 'what you miss depends on what you are watching for, not where it is',
  card: 'The famous gorilla comes from a 1999 paper by Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris, whose finer point often gets lost. They report that noticing an unexpected object depended on how similar it was to other things in the scene and on how hard the main task was. Being physically close to what you were attending to did not seem to help. Attention, they suggest, goes to objects and events rather than places.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Simons and Chabris report a study of inattentional blindness for complex objects and events in dynamic scenes. They say the likelihood of noticing an unexpected object depends on its similarity to other objects in the display and on the difficulty of the primary monitoring task, while spatial proximity to attended locations does not appear to affect detection, which they take to suggest that observers attend to objects and events rather than spatial positions [[simons1999|abstract]].'],
  links: [['radiologist-gorilla', 'citedby', 'Fourteen years later: the same gorilla idea, tested on people who are experts at looking.'], ['classroom-decorations', 'cousin', 'Attention in a very different setting: what a busy wall does to kindergarteners.']] },

{ id: 'ebbinghaus-again', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'checked', bib: 'murre2015', design: 'single-subject replication',
  head: 'a 22-year-old spent 70 hours memorising nonsense to check a result from 1880',
  card: 'Ebbinghaus’s forgetting curve rests largely on one man testing himself. So Jaap Murre and Joeri Dros did something charmingly similar: Dros, the only participant, spent about 70 hours learning lists of nonsense syllables and relearning them after delays from 20 minutes to 31 days. The curve came back, 135 years later, in Dutch. They also found it isn’t perfectly smooth. Most probably, it jumps upward at the 24-hour mark.',
  story: [
    'Few findings are as widely reproduced in textbooks, and as rarely reproduced in labs, as the forgetting curve. The method is punishing, which is why Ebbinghaus’s design, in which a single person learns nonsense until they can recite it, has so few direct replications [[murre2015|introduction]].',
    'Second author J. Dros, then 22, was the only subject [[murre2015|Subject]]. He learned syllable lists to a perfect criterion and relearned them after 20 minutes, 1 hour, 9 hours, 1, 2, 6 or 31 days, with savings (how much less practice the relearning took) as the measure [[murre2015|abstract; Procedure]]. With only about 75 days instead of Ebbinghaus’s seven months, each interval got 10 replications rather than his 12 to 45 [[murre2015|The Replication Experiment]].',
    'The authors conclude that the result replicates despite the century, the change of language and the different person. The most interesting wrinkle is that the curve probably isn’t smooth: savings seem to jump up at 24 hours [[murre2015|abstract]]. That is the kind of bump some earlier researchers attributed to sleep, though a design like this cannot establish why it happens [[murre2015|introduction]].',
    'The authors are the first to name the obvious limit: with one subject, it remains unclear what the shape of forgetting would be in anyone else [[murre2015|introduction]]. Still, two men, separated by more than a century, drawing nearly the same curve is a lovely thing to know about.'
  ],
  how: ['One participant; nonsense syllables built to fit Dutch phonotactics; each row learned to 100% correct before moving on; two or three lists on most days, with a maximum of four [[murre2015|Materials; Procedure]].'],
  links: [['sleepmore-seattle', 'cousin', 'If sleep may hide in the forgetting curve, what happens when a whole school district lets teenagers sleep more?'], ['method-of-loci', 'cousin', 'The flip side of forgetting: six weeks of training that made memories more durable.'], ['critical-period-grammar', 'cousin', 'A fun methodological opposite: where this study has one person, that one has about two-thirds of a million.']] },

{ id: 'method-of-loci', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'checked', bib: 'wagner2021', design: 'randomised training study (small groups)',
  head: 'six weeks of memory-palace practice more than tripled what people could recall a day later',
  card: 'Memory athletes swear by the method of loci, but do ordinary people benefit? Wagner and colleagues trained 17 novices for six weeks and compared them with two control groups. Delayed recall of a 72-word list rose from about 16 words to 56 in the trained group, while the two control groups reached only about 21 and 31. Four months later, trained participants still recalled around 50. The groups were small, but the gap is large.',
  story: [
    'The method of loci means placing things you want to remember along a route you know well, then mentally walking it. Wagner and colleagues wanted to know two things: whether it works for people with no special history, and what changes in the brain when it does [[wagner2021|abstract]].',
    'They compared 17 memory athletes, ranked among the world’s top 50, with matched controls. Separately, 50 novices were assigned to six weeks of method-of-loci training, six weeks of working memory training, or nothing, at 40 sessions of 30 minutes for the two active groups [[wagner2021|Study design]]. Participants studied 72 concrete nouns and were tested after 20 minutes and after 24 hours [[wagner2021|Methods]].',
    'Delayed recall in the trained group went from 16.1 words on average to 56.2. Working-memory-trained controls went from 19.4 to 30.5, and untrained controls from 18.5 to 21.4 [[wagner2021|Table 2]]. The key effect was on durable memories, the ones still there a day later, not on memories that fade fast. At four months the trained group’s immediate free recall was still about 50 words [[wagner2021|Table 2]].',
    'In the scanner, trained people and memory athletes both showed lower task-related activation in lateral prefrontal and parahippocampal regions, partly tied to better later performance, which the authors read as neural efficiency [[wagner2021|abstract]].',
    'Two honest wrinkles. Each group had only about 16 or 17 people, and assignment was pseudo-random, to balance baseline ability. And the authors flag that the four-month retest reused a word list from the pre-training session [[wagner2021|Limitations]].'
  ],
  how: ['Training study: n = 17 (memory training), 16 (active controls: working memory), 17 (passive controls). Athlete study: 17 athletes, 16 matched controls [[wagner2021|Table 1]].'],
  numbers: ['Group × memory type interaction: <em>F</em>(2, 94) = 30.85, <em>p</em> &lt; .0001; durable memories, trained &gt; active controls <em>t</em>(94) = 7.4 and &gt; passive controls <em>t</em>(94) = 8.84, both <em>p</em> &lt; .0001 [[wagner2021|Results]].'],
  links: [['ebbinghaus-again', 'cousin', 'The curve this technique pushes against, measured the hard way.'], ['aphantasia-profile', 'cousin', 'A technique built on imagined routes raises a question neither paper tested: what about people who report no visual imagery?'], ['testing-effect', 'cousin', 'A far less glamorous way to make memories durable: test yourself.']] },

{ id: 'aphantasia-profile', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'checked', bib: 'dawes2020', design: 'online questionnaire comparison',
  head: 'people with no mind’s eye also report fewer dreams, but spatial thinking seems fine',
  card: 'Some people report being unable to picture anything at all. Alexei Dawes and colleagues surveyed 267 of them, with two comparison groups. Aphantasia, they report, often extends past vision to other senses. It also comes with less vivid autobiographical memories and imagined futures, and fewer dreams at night. Yet reported spatial abilities looked unaffected. And there was no clear overall protection from trauma symptoms, which some had speculated about. All of this is self-report.',
  story: [
    'Most of us assume the inner movie is standard equipment. Aphantasia is a useful reminder of how wrong that can be, and of how little anyone can tell about another person’s mental life by asking casually.',
    'The team compared 267 aphantasic participants, recruited through online communities, with 203 age-matched controls from Mechanical Turk and a second control group of 193 undergraduates [[dawes2020|Participants]].',
    'Aphantasic participants reported reduced imagery in other senses too, although not all reported a complete lack, plus less vivid and less rich autobiographical memories and imagined futures [[dawes2020|abstract]]. They reported significantly fewer night dreams, and less awareness and control within dreams. Daydreaming was much murkier: the Bayesian evidence for a difference was inconclusive [[dawes2020|Day and night dream results]].',
    'One idea was that lacking intrusive imagery might protect people after stressful events. Overall scores offered no support (<em>p</em> = .776, BF<sub>10</sub> = 0.12), although some evidence favoured differences on particular subscales [[dawes2020|Trauma response results]].',
    'The authors suggest imagery may be a normal tool for building memories and imagined events, one that some minds do without. Caution matters here. These are self-reports, and the aphantasic group came from people who found their way to communities devoted to the subject, so it is a telling portrait but not a population estimate.'
  ],
  how: ['Final groups: aphantasia n = 267 (48% female, 83% with English as first language), MTurk controls n = 203, undergraduate controls n = 193. Strict significance threshold of <em>p</em> &lt; .0002 for group comparisons [[dawes2020|Participants; figures]].'],
  links: [['russian-blues', 'cousin', 'Different minds, different mental furniture: this time, the words available to you.'], ['method-of-loci', 'cousin', 'A memory technique built on mental imagery. Neither paper tested the obvious combination.']] },

{ id: 'russian-blues', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'checked', bib: 'winawer2007', design: 'reaction-time experiment',
  head: 'russian has two words for blue, and it shows up in milliseconds',
  card: 'Russian forces a choice between goluboy (lighter blue) and siniy (darker blue). Jonathan Winawer and colleagues found Russian speakers were faster to tell two blues apart when they fell on opposite sides of that boundary. English speakers, looking at the same squares, showed no such edge. And when Russian speakers silently rehearsed digits, the advantage vanished. A spatial task did not remove it, which suggests that language is taking part in the moment.',
  story: [
    'Whether language shapes perception has an embarrassing history of grand claims and weak tests. Earlier evidence mostly involved memory or subjective similarity, and critics could say those are hardly perception at all [[winawer2007|introduction]]. So this study asked for something simple and objective: pick which of two squares matches a third, as fast as you can.',
    'Twenty-six Russian speakers and 24 English speakers in the Boston area did this with blues spanning the goluboy/siniy border, either with no distraction, a spatial task, or a verbal one [[winawer2007|Methods]].',
    'For difficult, perceptually close pairs, Russian speakers were faster when the colours crossed the category line: 1,164 versus 1,288 ms with no interference, and the benefit remained under spatial interference (1,162 versus 1,270 ms). Under verbal interference it disappeared, even trending the other way. English speakers showed no category advantage in any condition [[winawer2007|Detailed Analyses]].',
    'That dual-task pattern is the clever bit. If Russian speakers’ visual systems had simply been permanently tuned by experience, a verbal task should not matter. Instead the authors argue that language is being used online, as the task happens [[winawer2007|abstract]].',
    'Keep the size in view. This is a difference of about a tenth of a second in a speeded task, on hard trials. It does not say Russians see a different sky. Three English and five Russian speakers were also excluded for losing too many trials, leaving 21 in each group [[winawer2007|Analysis]].'
  ],
  numbers: ['Main effect of distance: Russian speakers 926 vs 1,245 ms, <em>F</em>(1, 20) = 267, <em>p</em> &lt; .001; near-colour category advantage without interference <em>t</em>(20) = 2.59, <em>p</em> = .0176; with spatial interference <em>t</em>(20) = 2.18, <em>p</em> = .041 [[winawer2007|Detailed Analyses]].'],
  links: [['critical-period-grammar', 'cousin', 'Language again, from the other direction: not what it does to perception, but how long you can keep acquiring it.'], ['aphantasia-profile', 'cousin', 'Another look at how differently people’s minds can be furnished.'], ['radiologist-gorilla', 'cousin', 'Another case where seeing turns out to be less automatic than it feels.']] },

{ id: 'critical-period-grammar', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'checked', bib: 'hartshorne2018', design: 'massive online cross-sectional quiz, modelled',
  head: 'a viral grammar quiz suggests your language-learning ability holds up until about 17',
  card: 'Children outlearn adults at languages, but when does that edge fade? Joshua Hartshorne, Joshua Tenenbaum and Steven Pinker got an answer from a viral online quiz taken by 669,498 people. Their model puts the drop in grammar-learning ability at about 17.4 years, far later than commonly supposed. There’s a catch: to reach native-level proficiency, learners still appeared to need to start by around 10 to 12. Even native speakers kept improving until about 30.',
  story: [
    'The trouble with “younger is better” is that people who start young also get more years of practice. Hartshorne and colleagues point out a neat trap: a person’s final level alone can’t reveal underlying learning ability, because very different ability curves can produce the same final results [[hartshorne2018|1.1]].',
    'Their solution needed an absurd amount of data. Hence a grammar quiz with an irresistible hook: it guessed your native language and dialect. It was shared more than 300,000 times on Facebook [[hartshorne2018|Procedure]]. With 669,498 respondents, a computational model could disentangle current age, age at first exposure and years of experience [[hartshorne2018|abstract]].',
    'The best-fitting model has learning rate holding up, then declining from about 17.4 years [[hartshorne2018|Results]]. Alternatives with no change or a one-off step fit noticeably worse. But learning a language is so slow that a late start still costs you: traditional analyses here suggested learners must begin by 10–12 for native-level proficiency [[hartshorne2018|Comparison with previous results]]. And native speakers did not reach their plateau until around age 30, which is a nice thought to take away [[hartshorne2018|Results]].',
    'The major caveats: this is grammar specifically, not vocabulary or accent. It comes from one cross-sectional, self-selected internet sample, so developmental trajectories are inferred by a model rather than watched in individuals.'
  ],
  numbers: ['Best model <em>R</em>² = .89, with a rate change at 17.4 years; no-change model <em>R</em>² = .66; step-function model <em>R</em>² = .70 [[hartshorne2018|Results]].'],
  links: [['ebbinghaus-again', 'cousin', 'The opposite design choice: one meticulously tested person rather than two-thirds of a million quiz-takers.'], ['russian-blues', 'cousin', 'Another study asking what language does to the mind.'], ['many-labs-2', 'cousin', 'A different way to buy statistical precision: not one big quiz, but many labs running the same protocols.']] },

{ id: 'false-memories-reversed', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'checked', bib: 'oeberst2021', design: 'preregistered interview experiment',
  head: 'implanted false memories can be undone, without erasing the true ones',
  card: 'After three interviews, many students “remembered” childhood events their parents confirmed had never happened: 27% with light suggestion, 56% with heavy suggestion. Aileen Oeberst and colleagues then tried something modest, just telling people where memories can come from, including family stories and memory interviews themselves. False memory rates fell back to their starting levels, while true memories largely held. A year later they were down to 5%.',
  story: [
    'The false memory literature has been very good at showing that it can be done. It has been much less good at showing it can be undone, especially in ways a real investigator could use, since most reversal studies simply tell participants which event was fake [[oeberst2021|introduction]].',
    'Parents supplied two true negative childhood events and two plausible events that definitely did not happen. Students, none of them in psychology, sat through three weekly memory interviews about all four, under minimal or massive suggestion [[oeberst2021|Results]]. False memories grew to 27% and 56% respectively [[oeberst2021|abstract]].',
    'Then came two gentle techniques. Source sensitisation asked people to consider where a memory might have come from, such as family stories. False memory sensitisation, from a new interviewer, explained that repeated interviews can themselves create memories. Neither technique pointed to any particular event. Rates dropped back to first-interview levels (about 15% and 25%), while true events stayed high and were much less affected. A year later, after debriefing, false memories were down to 5% [[oeberst2021|abstract]].',
    'A rather moving detail: after debriefing, 44 participants (85%) thought they had simply forgotten the false events, in other words they were not lying to the interviewers about remembering [[oeberst2021|Discussion]].',
    'It is one small preregistered experiment (target n = 56), with plausible negative events and parents who could vouch for the truth. Real forensic memories come with no such ground truth, so the leap to courtrooms is promising rather than proven.'
  ],
  links: [['lost-in-the-mall-again', 'idea', 'Both descend from Loftus and Pickrell’s 1995 “lost in the mall” study, and both reference lists contain it. This one re-runs the classic itself.'], ['illusory-truth-implausible', 'cousin', 'Repeating things changes belief, too. Possibly most for the unlikely ones.'], ['moral-decline', 'cousin', 'Another memory bias with consequences: maybe everyone’s sense that people used to be nicer.']] },

{ id: 'lost-in-the-mall-again', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'abstract', bib: 'murphy2023', design: 'preregistered replication',
  head: 'getting lost in a mall, redux: a classic false memory study holds up',
  card: 'The “lost in the mall” study is still cited in legal cases. Gillian Murphy and colleagues repeated it with five times the sample and a preregistered plan. They report coding 35% of 123 participants as having a false memory of being lost in a mall as a child, compared with 25% originally. Mock jurors, they add, were also highly likely to believe the fabricated event had occurred and that the person truly remembered it.',
  story: ['From the abstract: the authors directly replicated Loftus and Pickrell (1995), raising the sample fivefold and preregistering detailed analysis plans. Participants (N = 123) discussed real and fabricated childhood events in a survey and two interviews, based on information from an older relative. The team reports coding 35% of participants as having a false memory for getting lost in a mall, compared with 25% in the original, and says mock jurors were highly likely to believe the fabricated event had occurred [[murphy2023|abstract]]. The publisher removed a duplicate copy of this paper, and this is the version it kept [[murphy2023|Crossref update record]].'],
  links: [['false-memories-reversed', 'idea', 'Both reference lists contain Loftus and Pickrell (1995). This one asks the natural next question: can such memories be taken back?'], ['illusory-truth-implausible', 'cousin', 'Another route to believing things that did not happen: hearing them again.']] },

{ id: 'illusory-truth-implausible', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'abstract', bib: 'repetition2026', design: 'online experiment', isNew: true,
  head: 'repetition may raise belief most in the things you least believed',
  card: 'You might hope that repetition only nudges things you already half-believe. In a large online experiment, Reed Orchinik and colleagues measured each person’s prior belief in specific trivia statements, then tested repetition’s effect. They report that the illusory truth effect was meaningful for statements people initially doubted, and stronger for those than for plausible ones. They read this as a warning about repeated falsehoods. Note that these were trivia statements.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Orchinik, Rand, Pennycook and Fazio tested the link between the illusory truth effect and prior belief in trivia statements, with an individual-level measure of how much each person believed each statement. They report the effect was meaningful for low-prior-belief statements and stronger for them, and conclude that repetition can be particularly harmful when statements are initially seen as implausible [[repetition2026|abstract]]. These are trivia statements, so this does not automatically transfer to politics or health.'],
  links: [['false-memories-reversed', 'cousin', 'From believing a statement to remembering an event: another way the mind can be walked somewhere false.'], ['ai-petitions', 'cousin', 'A different question about changing minds: when belief shifts, does behaviour follow?']] },

{ id: 'bionic-reading', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'abstract', bib: 'bolding2026', design: 'registered report with eye tracking', isNew: true, note: 'null result',
  head: 'bolding the start of each word moves your eyes, but doesn’t help you read',
  card: 'Apps that bold the first letters of words promise faster reading. Adam Parker and colleagues tracked the eye movements of 90 skilled readers in a registered report. They report that bolding did shift where eyes first landed in longer words. But that was all: there was no evidence of differences in word skipping, reading times or comprehension. Note this concerns skilled adult readers, not everyone such tools are marketed to.',
  story: ['From the abstract: in a registered report building on two pilot studies, Parker, Wang, Lee and Joseph compared start-bolded and middle-bolded text with unbolded text. They report that selective bolding shifted initial landing positions toward the start of longer words, as predicted, but found no evidence for differences in word skipping, word or sentence reading times, or comprehension, and conclude it does not facilitate more efficient word identification or overall reading in skilled adults [[bolding2026|abstract]].'],
  links: [['fonts-fortune', 'cousin', 'Another typographic promise. This one claimed that harder-to-read fonts help you remember.'], ['sans-forgetica', 'cousin', 'A font literally designed to aid memory, and what happened when it was tested.']] },

{ id: 'caffeine-search', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'abstract', bib: 'caffeine2026', design: 'single-blind placebo experiment', isNew: true, note: 'mostly null',
  head: 'coffee didn’t sharpen most of attention, at least in these tasks',
  card: 'Caffeine feels like the obvious attention upgrade. Sojung Youn and colleagues gave participants 200 mg of caffeine or a placebo before several visual search tasks. They report strong effects of colour-based statistical learning and effort trade-offs, but none of those was influenced by caffeine. The one benefit was faster target recognition, in the first experiment only. It suggests a limited role, and only for this kind of attentional selection.',
  story: ['From the abstract: in a single-blind between-subjects design, participants received 200 mg of caffeine or placebo. Target selection and distractor rejection were strongly influenced by colour-based statistical learning, and participants were sensitive to physical versus attentional effort, but the authors report that none of these was influenced by caffeine. A benefit on the speed of target recognition appeared in Experiment 1 only [[caffeine2026|abstract]].'],
  links: [['testosterone-crt', 'cousin', 'Another pill-versus-placebo test of a tidy story about chemistry and the mind.'], ['bionic-reading', 'cousin', 'Another everyday cognitive “upgrade” that did not do much under controlled conditions.']] },

{ id: 'testosterone-crt', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'abstract', bib: 'testost2026', design: 'double-blind placebo-controlled RCT', isNew: true, note: 'null result',
  head: 'testosterone did not make 1,000 men worse at trick questions',
  card: 'An earlier study suggested a testosterone dose impaired performance on the Cognitive Reflection Test, those riddles with a tempting wrong answer. Follow-ups disagreed. So, as an adversarial collaboration, Erik Knight, Gideon Nave and colleagues ran a preregistered, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial with 1,000 men. They report no significant effect, with the point estimate leaning the other way from the original hypothesis. It is a nice example of rivals testing a dispute together.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Knight and colleagues describe an unprecedentedly large, preregistered, double-blind, placebo-controlled experiment with 1,000 men, conducted as an adversarial collaboration. Participants received one dose of intranasal testosterone or placebo, completed the CRT and rated their confidence. The authors report an insignificant treatment effect on the CRT, with the point estimate opposite to the original hypothesis [[testost2026|abstract]].'],
  links: [['caffeine-search', 'cousin', 'A placebo-controlled test of a much more everyday chemical.'], ['many-smiles', 'idea', 'Another adversarial collaboration, this time trying to settle whether smiling makes you happier.']] },

{ id: 'attention-and-inflammation', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'abstract', bib: 'immune2026', design: 'preregistered within-subject experiments', isNew: true,
  head: 'where you direct your attention may change how your skin inflames',
  card: 'Distraction can dull pain. Nofar Mizrachi, Menachem Rottem and Liron Rozenkrantz asked whether attention reaches further, into the immune response. Across three preregistered experiments using acute skin inflammation, they report that attending to bodily sensations, rather than being distracted, produced smaller and better-regulated responses: about 1.5-fold smaller, in around 90% of people. It’s a striking claim about a local, short-lived reaction. It does not show that attention treats illness.',
  story: ['From the abstract: across three preregistered within-subjects experiments with acute skin inflammation as a model, the authors report that directing attention towards bodily sensations, compared with distraction, produced more regulated immune responses, consistent in roughly 90% of participants across two cohorts and about 1.5-fold smaller under internal attention. They propose both a sensory-dependent route and a top-down route engaging parasympathetic vagal activity [[immune2026|abstract]]. The “does not treat illness” caution is mine, not a finding of the study: the authors studied one local response in a controlled setting.'],
  links: [['open-label-placebo', 'cousin', 'Another study where a mental framing seems to reach the body, or at least reports about it.'], ['radiologist-gorilla', 'cousin', 'What attention does to perception, as a contrast to what it might do to immunity.']] },

{ id: 'open-label-placebo', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'checked', bib: 'kaptchuk2010', design: 'randomised controlled trial', adjacent: true,
  head: 'patients were told their pill was a placebo, and they still reported feeling better',
  card: 'Do placebos have to be secret? Ted Kaptchuk and colleagues randomly assigned 80 people with irritable bowel syndrome to no treatment or to pills openly labelled as placebo, with a persuasive explanation of why they might help. After three weeks, the placebo group reported significantly more improvement. It’s an astonishing setup with a modest evidence base: a small sample, self-reported outcomes, and possibly disappointed controls. The authors themselves say replication is essential.',
  story: [
    'Doctors are told that giving a placebo without disclosure may undermine trust [[kaptchuk2010|introduction]]. So here is a different question: what if nobody is deceived at all?',
    'Eighty patients were randomised, 37 to open-label placebo and 43 to no treatment [[kaptchuk2010|Table 1]]. The placebo group was told the pills were inert, like sugar pills, but also that placebo pills have been shown in clinical testing to produce “mind-body self-healing processes” [[kaptchuk2010|Methods]]. So this tests honesty about the contents plus a confident pitch, not a neutral description.',
    'On the main global improvement scale, scores were higher with open-label placebo at 11 days (5.2 vs 4.0, <em>p</em> &lt; .001) and at 21 days (5.0 vs 3.9, <em>p</em> = .002). Symptom severity and adequate relief also favoured placebo, while quality of life only trended (<em>p</em> = .08) [[kaptchuk2010|abstract; Results]].',
    'The authors list the limits themselves: it was a small trial, report bias (“wishing to please the experimenter”) is plausible, and people assigned to no treatment may have been disappointed, which would inflate the gap [[kaptchuk2010|Limitations]]. All main outcomes are self-reported, so this tells us what people reported rather than what changed in their guts. That the study needs replication before clinical use is their sentence too.'
  ],
  links: [['attention-and-inflammation', 'cousin', 'Another attempt to trace a route from mental framing into the body, with a physiological measure this time.'], ['active-learning-feels-worse', 'cousin', 'The reverse situation: what people report about their progress can diverge from an objective test.']] },

{ id: 'prospect-theory-global', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'abstract', bib: 'ruggeri2020', design: 'multinational direct replication', replicates: true,
  head: 'prospect theory, tested again across 19 countries, mostly survives',
  card: 'Not every classic falls over. Kai Ruggeri and colleagues re-ran the original 1979 prospect theory questions with 4,098 people in 19 countries and 13 languages, changing only the currencies. They report that 94% of items replicated, with some weakening, and that 12 of 13 theoretical contrasts held up. Variation between countries, they say, leaves plenty to explain. Their summary is that the empirical foundations replicate “beyond any reasonable thresholds”.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Ruggeri and colleagues directly tested Kahneman and Tversky’s original methods, adjusting only for current and local currencies and requiring all participants to respond to all items. They report replication for 94% of items with some attenuation, 12 of 13 theoretical contrasts replicating, and meaningful heterogeneity between countries and within individuals [[ruggeri2020|abstract]].'],
  links: [['many-labs-2', 'cites', 'Part of the replication story this paper positions itself against, and a study it cites.'], ['nature-science-replications', 'cites', 'It also cites this audit of social science in the top two journals.']] },

{ id: 'koriat-cues', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'checked', bib: 'koriat1997', design: 'paired-associate experiments', classic: true,
  head: 'studying something twice helps memory more than you’d guess',
  card: 'How do you know you’ve learned something? Asher Koriat argued in 1997 that you never read memory directly: you infer it from cues. In his first experiment, a second study presentation raised recall by 30% but people’s predictions by only 16%. Other things, like how hard an item feels, affect predictions strongly. One telling result: someone else’s predictions forecast your recall about as well across trials, while your own kept improving.',
  story: [
    'Koriat’s proposal was that judgments of learning are inferences, not readouts. They draw on intrinsic cues (properties of the item), extrinsic cues (study conditions such as repetition) and mnemonic cues (internal experiences like ease of processing) [[koriat1997|pp. 349–351]].',
    'Across four paired-associate experiments, item difficulty affected judgments about as much as recall, but extrinsic factors were discounted. A second study presentation raised recall by 30% and judgments by 16% [[koriat1997|Exp. 1, p. 354]]; longer presentation (2–8 s) raised recall by 10% and judgments by just 4% [[koriat1997|Exp. 4, p. 362]].',
    'With practice, an intriguing split emerged: people became increasingly underconfident, yet better at telling which items they would remember, with the judgment–recall gamma rising from .66 to .89 over four presentations [[koriat1997|Exp. 2, p. 358]]. Another participant’s judgments predicted a learner’s recall about equally well across presentations (.63, .59, .62, .58), while the learner’s own improved, as if experience gradually replaced shared intuitions about the items [[koriat1997|Exp. 2, p. 359]].',
    'That framework has become the default way psychologists talk about how we monitor our own minds. If you enjoy this, the pictures story below shows it doing work, 27 years on.'
  ],
  links: [['pictures-mislead', 'citedby', 'A 2024 paper uses the cue-utilization framework to explain why pictures can throw off students’ self-assessments.'], ['judgments-drive-study', 'idea', 'The next question: do those inferences actually change what people choose to study?'], ['fluent-lecturer', 'cousin', 'A very visible cue that misleads: a smooth speaker.']] },

{ id: 'surname-frequency', cat: 'cognitive', level: 'checked', bib: 'oppenheimer2004', design: 'frequency judgment experiments', classic: true,
  head: 'famous names can feel rarer, because you know why they came to mind',
  card: 'Easily recalled things usually seem common. But Stanford students rated famous surnames such as Bush and Nixon as more frequent than matched, actually rarer non-famous names only about a third of the time. Daniel Oppenheimer’s explanation: when you have an obvious reason for a name’s coming to mind, you discount it. People similarly underestimated how common their own surname was. Your mind seems to audit its own feelings of ease.',
  story: [
    'The availability heuristic says that what comes to mind easily seems frequent. Oppenheimer asked what happens when you know exactly why something came to mind.',
    'Students judged famous names as the more frequent option only 33.7% of the time, against matched names that were actually rarer (χ²(1, N = 350) = 33.32, <em>p</em> &lt; .001) [[oppenheimer2004|Exp. 1, p. 102]]. They underestimated the frequency of their own surname (mean score 1.8, versus −0.2 for yoked controls) [[oppenheimer2004|Exp. 2, p. 103]]. And after writing their initials for a “numerology” item, they underestimated how common those letters are, while people prompted with their birthdays overestimated theirs (115.2 vs −51.8) [[oppenheimer2004|Exp. 3, p. 104]].',
    'One small oddity: a couple of degrees of freedom in Experiment 2 do not match the reported samples, and the paper does not explain why [[oppenheimer2004|Exp. 2, p. 103]]. It doesn’t overturn anything, but it is the kind of thing a careful reader notices.'
  ],
  links: [['effort-feels-like-failing', 'idea', 'A conceptual parallel, not a citation link: both show that how you interpret a cognitive experience changes your judgment.'], ['koriat-cues', 'idea', 'Another view of judgments as inferences from cues, not direct readouts.']] },

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ social
{ id: 'conversations-end', cat: 'social', level: 'checked', bib: 'mastroianni2021', design: 'survey and lab observation',
  head: 'almost no conversation ends when both people want it to',
  card: 'Adam Mastroianni and colleagues asked people when they’d wanted a conversation to end, and when they thought their partner had. Among pairs of strangers, conversations ended when both people wanted just 1.59% of the time, and when one did only 29.37% of the time. People misjudged their partner’s desired stopping point by about 9 minutes on average. Ending a conversation, the authors suggest, is a coordination problem nobody has the information to solve.',
  story: [
    'Surprisingly, nobody had measured this. Study 1 asked 806 online participants about a recent conversation, usually with a romantic partner, friend or relative. Two-thirds (66.51%) said there had been a point where they’d felt ready for it to end [[mastroianni2021|Study 1]]. Those people enjoyed it less, and most agreed it would have been better to end there, which helps show they mean it [[mastroianni2021|Validation]].',
    'Study 2 was the better test, because you can only study coordination if you have both people. Strangers talked in the lab, then each reported separately (252 participants after exclusions). In a mere 1.59% of conversations did both want to stop at the same time and manage to do so; in 29.37%, one person’s wish matched the end. The conversation did not split the difference either [[mastroianni2021|When did conversations end?]]. Averaged across both studies, the gap between wanted and actual duration was around half of the conversation [[mastroianni2021|abstract]].',
    'Participants were also poor at guessing their partners’ wishes, missing by an average of 9.38 minutes [[mastroianni2021|Study 2]]. And the mismatch goes both ways: people wanted more as well as less.',
    'There’s a limit worth stating. These are retrospective self-reports, and “wanting to end” is hard to pin down, though the authors tried to validate it. Then there’s the lovely accidental finding: 57 of the 183 pairs of strangers talked for the full 45 minutes allowed, until the experimenter had to stop them [[mastroianni2021|Study 2]]. The authors’ conclusion is that most conversations end when no one wants them to.'
  ],
  links: [['ghosting-hurts-ghoster', 'cousin', 'Another gap between what two people in a conversation actually feel, this time when one stops replying.'], ['liking-gap', 'cousin', 'Another conversational illusion: after a chat, people like you more than you think.'], ['moral-decline', 'cousin', 'Same first author. Another widely shared belief about other people that the evidence does not back.']] },

{ id: 'ghosting-hurts-ghoster', cat: 'social', level: 'checked', bib: 'ghosting2026', design: 'preregistered recall, scenario and live studies', isNew: true,
  head: 'the person who ghosted you may feel worse than you think, up to a point',
  card: 'If someone has stopped answering your message, you probably imagine they don’t care. Sophia Li, Coral Zheng and Juliana Schroeder find that people recalling or imagining ghosting did underestimate how bad ghosters felt, especially when the silence was unintentional. In their live study, though, the overall gap vanished; it showed up only among those who saw the silence as ghosting. A one-line “sorry, can’t talk” shrank the misprediction too.',
  story: [
    'People already underestimate how good others feel when they get gratitude, compliments or support. This paper asks about the inverse: do people also underestimate how bad someone feels after doing something mildly antisocial [[ghosting2026|introduction]]?',
    'Study 1 asked 195 people to recall either the last time they failed to reply to someone or the last time someone failed to reply to them. Ghosters reported feeling worse than ghostees assumed: 2.27 vs 1.56 on a five-point scale (<em>d</em> = 0.77). The gap was larger when ghosting was seen as unintentional (2.62 vs 1.50, <em>d</em> = 1.17) [[ghosting2026|Table 1]].',
    'Study 2 used imagined scenarios (N = 601) and found a big gap for ghosting (<em>d</em> = 1.16) and a smaller one when people imagined a short reply (<em>d</em> = 0.49) [[ghosting2026|Table 1]].',
    'Study 3 is the one to dwell on. With a real interaction and 350 people, ghosters and the ghosted did not differ overall (3.26 vs 3.35, <em>d</em> = 0.07, <em>p</em> = .357). The misprediction appeared among people who considered the lack of response to be ghosting (<em>d</em> = 0.43) [[ghosting2026|Table 1]]. So the headline is real but conditional: the effect is strongest in memory and imagination, and more conditional in a live encounter. I like the paper more for reporting it so plainly.'
  ],
  how: ['All three studies were preregistered, with deviations listed in a supplement. Study 1’s final sample of 195 gave 80% power to detect <em>d</em> = 0.40. Median time since last message in Study 1 was 10 days, and most recalled ghostings involved friends (46.2%) or acquaintances (34.4%) [[ghosting2026|Study 1, Method]].'],
  links: [['undervaluing-gratitude', 'cites', 'The paper’s starting point, from its reference list: people misjudge the good feelings as well.'], ['liking-gap', 'cites', 'Also cited here: the original evidence that we underestimate how much new acquaintances like us.'], ['conversations-end', 'cousin', 'Conversations again, and two people who do not know what the other wants.']] },

{ id: 'liking-gap', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'boothby2018', design: 'lab and field studies',
  head: 'after a conversation, they probably liked you more than you think',
  card: 'Erica Boothby and colleagues call it the liking gap. They report that after talking, people systematically underestimated how much their partners liked them and enjoyed their company. It appeared among strangers in a lab, first-year students getting to know dorm mates, and members of the public at a workshop. It persisted over conversations of different lengths, and even for several months among dorm mates. Reassuring, if you tend to replay conversations afterwards.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Boothby, Cooney, Sandstrom and Clark report that following interactions, people systematically underestimated how much their conversation partners liked them and enjoyed their company. They observed it among strangers getting acquainted in the lab, first-year college students and dorm mates, and members of the general public at a personal development workshop; it persisted in conversations of varying lengths and lasted several months among dorm mates [[boothby2018|abstract]].'],
  links: [['ghosting-hurts-ghoster', 'citedby', 'A 2026 paper extends this kind of social misprediction to people who stop replying.'], ['stranger-scavenger', 'citedby', 'Can you learn your way out of such pessimism? A scavenger hunt says maybe.'], ['undervaluing-gratitude', 'cousin', 'The same genre of pleasant surprise: others react better than we forecast.']] },

{ id: 'undervaluing-gratitude', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'kumar2018', design: 'three experiments',
  head: 'you’re probably underrating that thank-you note',
  card: 'Amit Kumar and Nicholas Epley had people write gratitude letters, predict how recipients would react, and then asked the recipients. They report that writers underestimated how surprised and positive recipients would feel, and overestimated how awkward they’d be. Expected awkwardness and mood were both linked to how willing people were to send such a note. The worry you’ll seem weird, it suggests, may be keeping a good thing from happening.',
  story: ['From the abstract: in three experiments, participants wrote gratitude letters and predicted recipients’ surprise, happiness and awkwardness; recipients then reported their actual feelings. Kumar and Epley report that expressers underestimated how surprised recipients would be, overestimated how awkward they would feel and underestimated how positive they would feel, and that expected awkwardness and mood correlated with willingness to express gratitude [[kumar2018|abstract]].'],
  links: [['ghosting-hurts-ghoster', 'citedby', 'A paper citing this work, asking whether the same underestimation holds for bad feelings.'], ['liking-gap', 'cousin', 'Another pleasant error about what other people think of you.']] },

{ id: 'stranger-scavenger', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'sandstrom2022', design: 'week-long field intervention',
  head: 'a scavenger hunt made talking to strangers less scary',
  card: 'People avoid strangers partly because they expect rejection or awkwardness. Telling them otherwise hasn’t tended to work, say Gillian Sandstrom, Erica Boothby and Gus Cooney. So they made it a game: a week-long scavenger hunt involving repeatedly finding and talking to strangers. Compared with controls, they report, people grew less pessimistic about rejection and more confident in their conversation skills, and these benefits lasted at least a week beyond the study.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Sandstrom, Boothby and Cooney describe an intervention in which participants played a week-long scavenger hunt game involving repeatedly approaching and talking to strangers. They report that, compared with controls, it reduced pessimism about rejection and raised optimism about conversational ability, with benefits persisting at least a week after the study, and that daily reports showed expectations growing more positive and accurate by the day [[sandstrom2022|abstract]].'],
  links: [['liking-gap', 'cites', 'The reference list contains the liking gap, one reason such fears may be overblown.'], ['conversations-end', 'cousin', 'If you do start talking to a stranger: here is how badly you’ll coordinate the ending.']] },

{ id: 'moral-decline', cat: 'social', level: 'checked', bib: 'mastroianni2023', design: 'archival surveys plus original studies',
  head: 'people have been saying morals are declining for at least 70 years. the data disagree',
  card: 'In surveys from 60 nations going back seven decades, Adam Mastroianni and Daniel Gilbert find that people consistently report that morality is declining. But when people are asked about their contemporaries’ behaviour at the time, reports do not fall over the years. Stranger still, the decline shrinks or reverses when people rate those they know well, or those who lived before they were born. It suggests the decline may be a trick of biased exposure and memory.',
  story: [
    'The opening move is an inventory. Study 1 found 177 survey items asking 220,772 US respondents whether morality had changed. On 84.18% of them, most people said it had declined, and that share did not change with the year of the survey (Bayes factor 0.04, strong evidence of no trend) [[mastroianni2023|Study 1]]. Outside the US, 58 items across 59 nations and 354,120 people told the same story, on 86.21% of items [[mastroianni2023|Study 1]].',
    'They seem to mean it. People reported more decline over longer periods, and carved out exceptions where progress is plain, such as the treatment of people with physical disabilities or gay people. A supplementary study found the belief survived incentives for accuracy [[mastroianni2023|Study 1]]. People also blame both ageing and each new generation [[mastroianni2023|abstract]].',
    'But does morality actually decline? Study 4 turned to repeated surveys of how people currently rate others and behave themselves, with fairly concrete questions such as whether you had recently let a stranger go ahead of you in line. Over time, the answers do not change [[mastroianni2023|Study 4]].',
    'The proposed mechanism combines two known effects: people may encounter more negative than positive information about people in general, and memories of negative information may lose their emotional impact over time. The theory makes testable predictions, and two held up. Decline shrinks or reverses for people you know well (Study 5a, n = 283, on Mechanical Turk) or for those who lived before you were born [[mastroianni2023|Study 5]].',
    'Do keep in mind this rests on what people report about morality, since morality itself cannot be weighed. “Illusion” is the authors’ strong word, based on a strong pattern.'
  ],
  how: ['Total n across archival and original data: 12,492,983. Study 3 (n = 319) directly compared judgments of people in 2020 with 2005; Study 5a (n = 283) compared people in general with people in one’s personal world [[mastroianni2023|abstract; Studies 3 and 5a]].'],
  links: [['lost-wallets', 'cousin', 'Another surprising look at everyday honesty, this time with real wallets.'], ['conversations-end', 'cousin', 'Same first author, different everyday assumption, same deflating data.'], ['false-memories-reversed', 'cousin', 'How memory goes wrong, which the moral decline story depends on, studied directly.']] },

{ id: 'lost-wallets', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'cohn2019', design: 'field experiment in 40 countries',
  head: 'lost wallets came back more often when they had more money in them',
  card: 'What would you predict? Alain Cohn and colleagues turned in over 17,000 “lost” wallets at public and private institutions in 355 cities across 40 countries, varying how much money was inside, and tracked whether recipients contacted the owner. They report that in virtually all countries, wallets with more money were more likely to be returned. Neither laypeople nor professional economists predicted this. The authors point to altruism and not wanting to see oneself as a thief.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Cohn, Maréchal, Tannenbaum and Zünd report field experiments in 355 cities spanning 40 countries, turning in more than 17,000 lost wallets containing varying amounts of money. In virtually all countries, citizens were more likely to return wallets containing more money, and neither nonexperts nor professional economists predicted it. The authors say additional data suggest a combination of altruistic concerns and an aversion to viewing oneself as a thief [[cohn2019|abstract]].'],
  links: [['moral-decline', 'cousin', 'If you were expecting worse of strangers, this is the paper about why you might have been.'], ['moral-reminders', 'cousin', 'A very different test of what keeps people honest: reminders of the Ten Commandments.']] },

{ id: 'sign-first', cat: 'social', level: 'checked', bib: 'kristal2020', design: 'preregistered direct replication plus conceptual replications', note: 'failed replication',
  head: 'signing at the top of the form did not make people more honest',
  card: 'A famous 2012 finding said that signing an honesty statement before filling in a form, rather than after, reduced cheating. Governments took note. Then Ariella Kristal and colleagues, including original authors, failed to replicate it across five conceptual replications and one large preregistered direct replication (n = 1,235). They also point to an oddity in the original field data: the two groups’ odometer readings differed at baseline. The original paper was later retracted.',
  story: [
    'The original PNAS paper had a beguilingly practical idea: put the signature at the top, and ethics are salient exactly when they’re needed. It rested on two small lab experiments (n = 101 and 60) and an insurance field experiment (n = 13,488) [[kristal2020|introduction]].',
    'When Kristal and colleagues tried to apply the idea online, it kept not working. So, unusually and to their credit, they teamed up with the original authors. Five conceptual replications (total n = 4,559) found no effect [[kristal2020|abstract]]. Then came a direct replication of the original’s first lab study, preregistered and powered to detect <em>d</em> = 0.10 [[kristal2020|Results]].',
    'The result: no detectable effect of signing first on all three preregistered outcomes, with confidence intervals tightly around zero (<em>d</em> = 0.07, 0.04 and 0.06, all <em>p</em> values above .76) [[kristal2020|Results]].',
    'They also returned to the field data, noting that reported odometer readings in the two conditions were significantly different at baseline, and said the randomisation may have failed [[kristal2020|introduction]]. In 2021, PNAS published a retraction of the 2012 paper [[ariely2021|retraction notice]]. A 2026 correction to Kristal et al. fixes reporting errors in its tables and confidence intervals. The three effect sizes above match the corrected version [[kristal2020|Crossref update record]].',
    'It’s a good story about how science can correct itself, and a slightly uncomfortable one about how long a tidy intervention can circulate before it does.'
  ],
  links: [['sign-first-original', 'failed', 'The now-retracted original, available so you can see what was claimed.'], ['moral-reminders', 'cousin', 'Another much-cited honesty intervention that large replications did not support.'], ['lost-wallets', 'cousin', 'Honesty outside the lab, and an unexpected direction.']] },

{ id: 'sign-first-original', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'shu2012', design: 'lab and field experiments (retracted)', retracted: true,
  head: 'the retracted study claiming signatures at the top reduce cheating',
  card: 'This paper has been retracted, and it’s here for the historical record, not as evidence. Lisa Shu and colleagues claimed in 2012 that signing before rather than after an opportunity to cheat makes ethics salient and significantly reduces dishonesty. The claim failed to replicate in later work by the same group and others, and PNAS published a retraction in 2021. Treat it as the starting point of a cautionary tale.',
  story: ['This is a retracted article. Its abstract claimed that signing before rather than after the opportunity to cheat made ethics salient and significantly reduced dishonesty [[shu2012|abstract]]. Do not treat that claim as a finding. Retraction notice: [[ariely2021|retraction notice]].'],
  links: [['sign-first', 'failedby', 'The large replication effort that did not find the effect.'], ['moral-reminders', 'cousin', 'Another honesty intervention under replication.']] },

{ id: 'moral-reminders', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'verschuere2018', design: 'registered replication report', note: 'failed replication',
  head: 'remembering the ten commandments did not stop people cheating',
  card: 'In a much-cited 2008 experiment, recalling the Ten Commandments rather than books from school reduced cheating on a puzzle task (d = 0.48). Bruno Verschuere and colleagues pooled 25 direct replications following one preregistered protocol. In the main analysis, they report that people given a moral reminder claimed slightly more solved puzzles, 0.11 more, with a confidence interval that includes zero. Numerically, this is the opposite direction of the original.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Verschuere and colleagues report 25 direct replications (total N = 5,786) of Mazar, Amir and Ariely (2008, Experiment 1), which had reported that a moral reminder led to 1.45 fewer claimed matrices (d = 0.48). In the primary meta-analysis of 19 replications (n = 4,674), participants who could cheat reported solving 0.11 more matrices after a moral reminder (95% CI [−0.09, 0.31]), numerically opposite to the original effect [[verschuere2018|abstract]].'],
  links: [['sign-first', 'cousin', 'Another honesty nudge that large replications did not support.'], ['lost-wallets', 'cousin', 'And yet people return wallets. Real honesty may work differently.']] },

{ id: 'hungry-judges', cat: 'social', level: 'checked', bib: 'danziger2011', design: 'observational analysis of real rulings', classic: true,
  head: 'parole approvals seemed to fall from about 65% to nearly zero between meal breaks',
  card: 'Shai Danziger and colleagues tracked 1,112 rulings by eight Israeli judges. Favourable decisions dropped from about 65% at the start of a session to near zero, then bounced back after a food break. It became a favourite psychology anecdote. But it’s observational, and the interpretation is disputed: the authors treated case order as effectively random, and critics who interviewed court staff say it isn’t. Read the sequel before you repeat this one at dinner.',
  story: [
    'Legal realists have long joked that justice is what the judge ate for breakfast. Danziger, Levav and Avnaim-Pesso took that literally, or nearly [[danziger2011|abstract]].',
    'They coded 1,112 rulings over 50 days by eight judges, who heard 14–35 cases daily for about six minutes each, with two food breaks splitting each day into three sessions. A favourable ruling was the exception, with 64.2% of requests rejected overall [[danziger2011|Materials and Methods]].',
    'The pattern looks startling: approvals fell from about 65% to nearly zero across a session and returned to about 65% after a break [[danziger2011|abstract]]. The authors speculated that as decision-making wears on, judges lean toward the default of denying, and that breaks may restore mental resources through rest, mood or glucose, though they could not separate those options [[danziger2011|Materials and Methods]].',
    'Their case depended on the assumption that case order was effectively exogenous, and that’s exactly where it was challenged. Keep reading.'
  ],
  how: ['Ordinal position, time of day, seriousness of offence, previous incarcerations and months served were recorded. The figure of proportion favourable by position uses the first 95% of data in each session, because few sessions run long [[danziger2011|Materials and Methods; Results]].'],
  links: [['hungry-judges-challenge', 'challengedby', 'The direct reply: court staff say the order of cases is not random.'], ['ego-depletion-many-labs', 'idea', 'The idea of draining mental resources, tested head-on by 36 labs.']] },

{ id: 'hungry-judges-challenge', cat: 'social', level: 'checked', bib: 'weinshall2011', design: 'reanalysis and interviews',
  head: 'the hungry judge effect may partly be about who walks in last',
  card: 'Keren Weinshall-Margel and John Shapard looked into the parole study’s setup, and interviewed attorneys, a judge and prison staff. They were told cases aren’t ordered randomly. Prisoners without lawyers, about a third of cases, succeeded 15% of the time, compared with 35% with counsel, and attorneys tend to present their own group of cases together. So the pattern across a session may reflect the order, not just the judge’s stomach. That’s a good thing to know.',
  story: [
    'This is a short letter with a sharp point. The original conclusion, they note, depends on the order of cases being random, or at least unrelated to the timing of meal breaks [[weinshall2011|letter]].',
    'They examined the original authors’ data, obtained additional data from 12 hearing days (n = 227 decisions), and interviewed three attorneys, a parole panel judge and five staff members. They learned that case ordering is not random [[weinshall2011|letter]].',
    'Unrepresented prisoners made up about a third of cases and prevailed only 15% of the time, compared with 35% for those with counsel. On average 4.1 prisoners per day had “shared counsel”, and interviewees said an attorney usually presents all their cases together, in whatever order they choose [[weinshall2011|letter]]. Excluding deferred cases, success rates in the original data run from about 75% to 42% across a session, which is a much smaller swing than “near zero” [[weinshall2011|letter]].',
    'This doesn’t prove hunger plays no part. It says that an alternative explanation had not been ruled out. Those critics also suggest the original authors should have suspected as much.'
  ],
  links: [['hungry-judges', 'challenges', 'The original claim this letter disputes.'], ['marshmallow-trust', 'idea', 'A similar twist in a very different setting: what looks like a lapse of willpower may reflect the situation.']] },

{ id: 'ego-depletion-many-labs', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'vohs2021', design: 'multisite preregistered test (k = 36)', note: 'null result',
  head: 'does using self-control drain it? 36 labs didn’t find much',
  card: 'Ego depletion was among social psychology’s most influential ideas: self-control runs on a limited resource. After earlier challenges, a large team that included the theory’s proponents ran a preregistered test across 36 labs and 3,531 people. They report a nonsignificant effect (d = 0.06), with the data about 4 times more likely under no effect than under their informed hypothesis. Exploratory tests suggested it might be larger in people reporting more fatigue.',
  story: ['From the abstract: in a preregistered multilaboratory project (k = 36; N = 3,531), Vohs and colleagues report a nonsignificant confirmatory result (d = 0.06), with Bayesian analyses finding the data 4 times more likely under the null than the informed alternative. Exploratory analyses on the full sample found a significant d = 0.08, and exploratory moderator tests suggested a larger effect among people reporting more fatigue, but no moderation by trait self-control, willpower beliefs or action orientation [[vohs2021|abstract]].'],
  links: [['hungry-judges', 'idea', 'A real-world finding that was explained partly by depleted resources.'], ['deadlines-procrastination', 'cousin', 'Another classic self-control result, tried again in 2026.']] },

{ id: 'deadlines-procrastination', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'procrast2026', design: 'close replication', isNew: true, note: 'failed replication',
  head: 'evenly spaced deadlines may not beat procrastination after all',
  card: 'A 2002 study by Ariely and Wertenbroch is the classic evidence that imposed deadlines help people beat procrastination. Kyle Hyndman and Alberto Bisin ran a close replication with adults at a large US public university. They report that changing the deadlines had a negligible effect on the performance measures, and that evenly spaced, externally imposed deadlines did not stand out. It’s a small study, roughly twice the original’s size, and one setting.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Hyndman and Bisin report that their results do not replicate Study 2 of Ariely and Wertenbroch. Changes in deadlines had a negligible effect on three performance metrics and several survey metrics, and evenly spaced external deadlines did not stand out in reducing procrastination [[procrast2026|abstract]]. From the methods section I also checked the setup: a proofreading task with evenly spaced, self-imposed or no intermediate deadlines; about 120 participants targeted, versus 60 originally; and a nearly identical supplemental replication [[procrast2026|Method]].'],
  links: [['nature-science-replications', 'cites', 'Its reference list includes this audit of how often high-profile social science holds up.'], ['ego-depletion-many-labs', 'cousin', 'Another classic of self-regulation under renewed scrutiny.']] },

{ id: 'stereotype-threat-rrr', cat: 'social', level: 'checked', bib: 'johnsrrr2026', design: 'registered replication report across nine labs', isNew: true, note: 'failed replication',
  head: 'nine labs tried to reproduce stereotype threat in maths. the effect was close to zero',
  card: 'Johns and colleagues reported in 2005 that women underperformed on maths tests framed as measuring gender differences. Andrea Stoevenbelt and colleagues ran nine direct replications with 1,709 people. Among women, the effect was virtually null (g = 0.04, N = 1,275), against the original’s d = −0.82 in 45 people. The manipulation itself seemed to take. That casts doubt on this effect’s generality rather than settling every question about stereotypes and performance.',
  story: [
    'Stereotype threat, the fear of confirming a negative stereotype about your group, has become one of psychology’s best-known ideas. But the authors argue that the research has leaned on suboptimal designs and analyses, may be subject to publication bias, and has not much tested cross-cultural generality [[johnsrrr2026|abstract]]. So they picked a representative study and ran it nine times.',
    'The findings: no significant interaction between stereotype threat condition and gender. Women scored lower than men on the maths test only (<em>g</em> = −0.26), and the threat effect among women was <em>g</em> = 0.04, 95% CI [−0.11, 0.20], far from the original <em>d</em> = −0.82, CI [−1.45, −0.18] [[johnsrrr2026|abstract]].',
    'Importantly, the setup worked in the sense of the original checks: results on the manipulation checks were replicated [[johnsrrr2026|Results]]. People appear to have perceived the conditions differently. It’s just that their test scores didn’t follow.',
    'The authors flag a possible limitation: perhaps the tests weren’t difficult enough to elicit an effect. But average scores (7.82 and 8.66 out of 30) were similar to the original study’s 7.40, which makes this less likely as an escape hatch [[johnsrrr2026|Discussion]]. They conclude that the results cast doubt on the generalisability of this effect on women’s mathematics performance [[johnsrrr2026|abstract]], which is more careful than a claim that stereotypes never matter.'
  ],
  numbers: ['Gender gap on mathematics: <em>g</em> = −0.26, <em>SE</em> = 0.11, 95% CI [−0.47, −0.05]. Threat effect among women: <em>N</em> = 1,275, <em>g</em> = 0.04, <em>SE</em> = 0.08. Test reliability: α = .82 (easier) and .88 (regular). Secondary analyses found a main effect of gender composition and a random effect of lab on maths performance, but neither affected the threat × gender interaction [[johnsrrr2026|abstract; Discussion]].'],
  links: [['growth-mindset-national', 'cousin', 'Another social-psychological idea about belief and achievement, this one tested on a national scale with a modest but targeted result.'], ['learning-styles-kids', 'cousin', 'A popular belief about learning, tested in a school.'], ['many-labs-2', 'cousin', 'The wider picture of what happens when classic findings get re-run.']] },

{ id: 'many-labs-2', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'klein2018', design: 'preregistered multi-site replications', adjacent: true,
  head: 'twenty-eight findings, 125 samples, and about half of them replicated',
  card: 'Many Labs 2 ran preregistered replications of 28 classic and contemporary findings, with protocols reviewed in advance, across 125 samples and 15,305 people in 36 countries and territories. Using the usual significance test, Richard Klein and colleagues report 15 replicated (54%). Median effect sizes fell from 0.60 to 0.15. And the variation across settings was smaller than you might suspect: where effects were near zero overall, few varied significantly by sample.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Klein and colleagues report that 15 of 28 replications (54%) showed a statistically significant effect in the original direction at p < .05 and 14 (50%) at p < .0001. Median comparable Cohen’s ds were 0.60 for originals and 0.15 for replications; 21 replications had smaller effects than the originals. Heterogeneity across settings was significant in 11 (39%), mostly among the findings with the largest overall effects, and only 1 effect near zero in the aggregate showed significant heterogeneity [[klein2018|abstract]].'],
  links: [['nature-science-replications', 'cites', 'A related audit, of the top two general-science journals, that is in its reference list.'], ['prospect-theory-global', 'citedby', 'A counterexample that cites it: a classic that largely held up.'], ['many-smiles', 'citedby', 'A later collaboration citing it, set up to resolve one specific dispute.']] },

{ id: 'nature-science-replications', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'camerer2018', design: 'high-powered preregistered replications', adjacent: true,
  head: 'social science in nature and science: 13 of 21 replicated',
  card: 'Colin Camerer and colleagues replicated 21 experimental social science studies published in Nature and Science between 2010 and 2015, with samples averaging about five times the originals and plans reviewed by original authors. They report 13 significant effects in the same direction (62%), at about half the original effect size on average. And, the abstract adds, other researchers could anticipate this, since peer beliefs about replicability were strongly related to outcomes.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Camerer and colleagues report a significant effect in the same direction for 13 of 21 studies (62%), with effect sizes averaging about 50% of the originals, 12 (57%) to 14 (67%) studies by complementary indicators, and an estimated true-positive rate of 67% in a Bayesian analysis [[camerer2018|abstract]].'],
  links: [['many-labs-2', 'citedby', 'Many Labs 2 is in this paper’s reference list, and vice versa: the two projects ran alongside one another.'], ['deadlines-procrastination', 'citedby', 'A 2026 replication citing it, in the same spirit.']] },

{ id: 'smile-pen', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'strack1988', design: 'lab experiments', classic: true,
  head: 'the original pen-in-mouth study: does smiling make cartoons funnier?',
  card: 'Fritz Strack, Leonard Martin and Sabine Stepper wanted to test whether facial activity influences feelings, without telling people to smile. Their trick: have them hold a pen in their mouth in ways that facilitated or inhibited smiling muscles. They report that cartoons drew more intense humour responses in the facilitating position. A classic for decades, and the start of a long, public dispute. Follow the links, because it doesn’t end here.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Strack, Martin and Stepper had subjects hold a pen in their mouth in ways that inhibited or facilitated the muscles typically associated with smiling, without requiring them to pose a smile. They report more intense humour responses under facilitating conditions in Study 1, a replication in Study 2, and that facial feedback operated on the affective rather than the cognitive component of the humour response [[strack1988|abstract]].'],
  links: [['smile-rrr', 'failedby', '17 labs ran the same protocol, nearly three decades later.'], ['many-smiles', 'citedby', 'And later, a collaboration of former disagreers designed the test together.']] },

{ id: 'smile-rrr', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'wagenmakers2016', design: 'registered replication report (17 labs)', note: 'failed replication',
  head: 'seventeen labs held pens in their mouths. the difference: 0.03 points',
  card: 'The classic pen study reported a 0.82-point difference on a 10-point funniness scale. E.-J. Wagenmakers and colleagues gathered 17 independent direct replications following a single vetted protocol. Their meta-analysis found a difference of 0.03 points, with a 95% confidence interval from −0.11 to 0.16. It became one of the best-known failed replications in psychology. But it isn’t quite the end of the story about whether faces influence feelings.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Wagenmakers and colleagues report 17 independent direct replications of Study 1 of Strack et al. (1988). The meta-analysis found a rating difference of 0.03 units (95% CI [−0.11, 0.16]), compared with 0.82 units in the original [[wagenmakers2016|abstract]].'],
  links: [['smile-pen', 'failed', 'The 1988 original.'], ['many-smiles', 'citedby', 'What happened when both sides designed the next test together.']] },

{ id: 'many-smiles', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'coles2022', design: 'preregistered adversarial collaboration (19 countries)',
  head: 'smiling can make you feel happier. the pen, maybe not',
  card: 'After years of dispute, the Many Smiles Collaboration, including people on different sides, designed one test together: 3,878 people across 19 countries. Nicholas Coles and colleagues report that a facial mimicry task and a voluntary facial action task could both amplify and initiate feelings of happiness. But with the famous unobtrusive pen-in-mouth method, evidence was less conclusive. So a mixed answer, and perhaps a more useful one than “it’s true” or “it’s false”.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Coles and colleagues describe a global adversarial collaboration and preregistered multicentre study designed to specify the conditions most likely to produce facial feedback effects. Data from n = 3,878 participants across 19 countries indicated that mimicry and voluntary facial action tasks could amplify and initiate happiness; evidence was less conclusive when feedback was manipulated unobtrusively through a pen-in-mouth task [[coles2022|abstract]].'],
  links: [['smile-rrr', 'cites', 'The failed replication this collaboration had to reckon with.'], ['smile-pen', 'cites', 'The 1988 study that started the argument.'], ['testosterone-crt', 'idea', 'Another dispute taken on by an adversarial collaboration.']] },

{ id: 'infants-helpers', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'hamlin2007', design: 'infant preference experiments', classic: true,
  head: 'do babies prefer helpers? the study that started the question',
  card: 'J. Kiley Hamlin, Karen Wynn and Paul Bloom reported in 2007 that 6- and 10-month-olds prefer a character who helps another over one who hinders, with neutral characters in between. They took this as evidence that preverbal infants evaluate others by how they treat third parties, possibly a foundation for moral thought. It launched a whole research field. Do read the next link before telling anyone that babies are born moral.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Hamlin, Wynn and Bloom report that 6- and 10-month-old infants prefer an individual who helps another to one who hinders another, prefer a helper to a neutral individual, and prefer a neutral individual to a hinderer, and argue this capacity may serve as a foundation for moral thought and action [[hamlin2007|abstract]].'],
  links: [['infants-replication', 'failedby', 'Seventeen years later, 37 labs tested it together.'], ['overimitation', 'cousin', 'Another surprising look at what young children are actually doing when they watch adults.']] },

{ id: 'infants-replication', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'lucca2025', design: 'preregistered multi-lab replication', note: 'failed replication',
  head: '37 labs, 567 infants, and no preference for helpers',
  card: 'Using the ManyBabies framework, Kelsey Lucca and colleagues tested 1,018 infants at 37 labs on five continents, with 567 included in the analyses. They report that 49.34% preferred the helper in the social condition, not different from chance. A nonsocial control condition, with an inanimate object pushed up or down, also did not differ. They conclude that any effect is weaker, absent, or develops later than estimated. Tough, and very well done, work.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Lucca and colleagues report a preregistered multi-lab study testing 1,018 infants (567 included, aged 5.5–10.5 months) from 37 labs. Overall, 49.34% preferred Helpers over Hinderers in the social condition, and 55.85% preferred characters who pushed an inanimate object up rather than down in the nonsocial condition; neither proportion differed from chance or from each other. They interpret this as evidence against prosocial preferences in the hill paradigm [[lucca2025|abstract]]. A published correction changes one exploratory statistic, and says no conclusions are altered [[lucca2025|Crossref update record]].'],
  links: [['infants-helpers', 'failed', 'The 2007 original.'], ['many-labs-2', 'cousin', 'The big-team approach as it was first being used for classic findings in adults.']] },

{ id: 'crowd-of-sheep', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'sheep2026', design: 'preregistered replication and extension', isNew: true,
  head: 'most people admit they’re as conformist as everyone else',
  card: 'A 2007 study suggested people think they’re alone in a crowd of sheep. Kailey Plowman and colleagues replicated it with 287 students. On average, yes, people said they conform less than their peers. But look closer: 61.6% said they conformed just as much, and the average effect was driven by the 27.4% who said less. A replication that succeeded, while changing what the result means. Averages can quietly misdescribe most of the people in them.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Plowman, Smith, Monroe and Crawley preregistered a replication and extension (N = 287) of Pronin et al. (2007, Study 2; N = 40). The average judgment replicated, but 61.6% reported conforming as much as their peers and the effect was driven by the 27.4% who indicated they conformed less. A close friend was rated about the same as peers in the extension [[sheep2026|abstract]].'],
  links: [['selfish-distortions', 'cousin', 'More on the flattering stories people tell themselves, in a new set of experiments.'], ['liking-gap', 'cousin', 'Another gap between how people see themselves and how they are seen.']] },

{ id: 'selfish-distortions', cat: 'social', level: 'abstract', bib: 'selfish2026', design: 'nine preregistered online experiments', isNew: true,
  head: 'selfish and generous people both bend the facts, but selfish spin seems to spread',
  card: 'Marius Vollberg and Jörg Gross gave 8,769 mostly US adults choices whose effects on others were ambiguous. They report that selfish people played up the social good of their choice and played down the alternative, while prosocial people exaggerated their sacrifice. These distortions appeared even before choosing, or when choices were forced, which suggests more than after-the-fact justification. And distortions favouring selfishness appeared contagious: others adopted them, then acted more selfishly.',
  story: ['From the abstract: across nine preregistered online experiments, the authors report that selfish individuals inflated the social benefit of their own choice relative to the alternative (experiment 1a: b = 0.24, 95% CI 0.14–0.34), while prosocial individuals portrayed theirs as comparatively more beneficial and, less consistently, inflated their sacrifice. Selfish-serving distortions showed evidence of contagion (experiment 6: b = 0.49, 95% CI 0.10–0.88) [[selfish2026|abstract]].'],
  links: [['crowd-of-sheep', 'cousin', 'Another study of how people describe their own behaviour relative to others.'], ['ai-petitions', 'cousin', 'Another route by which conversation changes what people do.']] },

{ id: 'ai-petitions', cat: 'social', level: 'checked', bib: 'aipersuade2026', design: 'two large preregistered experiments', isNew: true,
  head: 'ai chats got many more people to sign a petition, without going through their attitudes',
  card: 'Much research shows chatbots can shift attitudes. Kobi Hackenburg and colleagues asked if that reaches behaviour. With 14,779 UK adults across two studies, AI conversations raised petition signing from 25.2% to 37.8% in one and from 21.1% to 40.4% in the other. But effects on attitudes and on behaviour weren’t correlated across conditions. And although information drives attitude change, the information-focused approach had the smallest effect on actions. If you judge persuasion only by attitudes, they argue, you may misread its real-world power.',
  story: [
    'The hand-wringing about AI persuasion has mostly been measured in survey responses. This paper asks about something with friction: signing an actual petition, or giving up earnings [[aipersuade2026|abstract]].',
    'Across two preregistered studies, 14,779 UK adults (17,950 responses) conversed with frontier models about one of eight causes [[aipersuade2026|Results]]. In Study 1, people who talked with the AI about the petition were 12.8 percentage points more likely to sign than controls (covariate-adjusted; observed 25.2% vs 37.8%). In Study 2 the gap was 19.7 points (observed 21.1% vs 40.4%), and they were 6.1 points more likely to allocate their earnings to the organisation behind the petition. These held across models and a run of robustness checks [[aipersuade2026|Results]].',
    'The interesting part is what doesn’t travel. Across randomised conditions, they found no evidence of a correlation between AI effects on attitudes and effects on behaviour. Information provision, which repeated earlier findings by driving attitude change, showed no such role for behaviour; among eight behavioural strategies, the information one had the smallest effect, though differences among them were small [[aipersuade2026|abstract]].',
    'Keep in mind these are behaviours inside an online study, right after a conversation with a persuasive agent, and a petition signature costs little. Whether these effects persist or matter outside such settings is a separate question.'
  ],
  links: [['ai-without-guardrails', 'cousin', 'A different AI worry: does help now cost you learning later?'], ['illusory-truth-implausible', 'cousin', 'A less interactive route to shifting belief: repetition.'], ['selfish-distortions', 'cousin', 'Another reminder that what people say and what they do can come apart.']] },

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ educational
{ id: 'active-learning-feels-worse', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'deslauriers2019', design: 'randomised classroom experiment',
  head: 'students learned more in active classes but felt they’d learned less',
  card: 'Louis Deslauriers and colleagues randomly assigned 149 Harvard physics students to the same material, taught either by an expert’s polished lecture or through active problem-solving. Those in active classes scored higher on a test of learning, but felt they’d learned less, and rated the lectures as better. It looks like the effort of struggling was read as a sign of not understanding. It’s awkward news for anyone relying on student evaluations.',
  story: [
    'Active learning’s benefits were already well documented. What was unclear was why students are often said to resist it, because studies rarely measured how they feel without an instructor promoting one approach or the other [[deslauriers2019|introduction]].',
    'The team toggled only this. Students were randomly assigned, in two courses and 149 students overall, to matched content and handouts, taught either through polished lectures emphasising fluency or through small-group problem-solving with instructor feedback. All students experienced both [[deslauriers2019|Methods]]. After each class they rated their feeling of learning, then took a multiple-choice test of learning [[deslauriers2019|Results]].',
    'Students in the active sessions learned more, but felt they had learned less. Passive-lecture students rated the instruction more highly and expressed a greater preference to have all their physics taught that way [[deslauriers2019|introduction; Results]]. In interviews with 17 of the students, the authors’ account took form: struggling through hard problems may make you painfully aware of what you don’t know, while a smooth lecture confirms an inflated sense of ability [[deslauriers2019|Results]].',
    'Two scope notes. This is one institution with strong physics students. And the “active” condition still featured an instructor explaining things about half the time [[deslauriers2019|Results]], so it isn’t “no teaching”. Still, as a reason to mistrust that good feeling after a great lecture, it is hard to beat.'
  ],
  links: [['fluent-lecturer', 'cites', 'An earlier demonstration, in its reference list: a smooth speaker raises confidence without raising learning.'], ['ai-tutor-harvard', 'citedby', 'A later study by overlapping authors, in which AI tutoring is compared with this very kind of active class.'], ['effort-feels-like-failing', 'idea', 'The same idea, experimentally dissected: students taking effort as a sign of poor learning.']] },

{ id: 'fluent-lecturer', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'carpenter2013', design: 'lab experiments',
  head: 'a smooth lecturer makes you feel you learned more. the test disagrees',
  card: 'Shana Carpenter and colleagues showed students a video of one instructor giving a talk either fluently, upright, with eye contact and no notes, or disfluently, slumped, hesitating, reading from notes. People who saw the fluent version predicted they would remember much more. But actual memory did not differ significantly between the groups. The polish moved their confidence and did nothing measurable for retention. Worth recalling at the next excellent keynote you attend.',
  story: [
    'In the fluent video, the instructor stood upright, kept eye contact and spoke without notes; in the disfluent one, the same instructor slumped, looked away and spoke haltingly with notes [[carpenter2013|Method]].',
    'People watched one of the two. In Experiment 1, they then predicted how much they would recall. Predictions were much higher after the fluent speaker, but actual performance did not differ (<em>t</em> &lt; 0.50) [[carpenter2013|Exp. 1]]. Experiment 2 gave participants a text script of the video to study before the test, and test performance again did not differ (.44 vs .40, <em>t</em>(68) = 0.81, <em>p</em> = .42) [[carpenter2013|Exp. 2]].',
    'The takeaway is narrow but useful: a speaker’s smoothness can act as a cue to learning without being one. It doesn’t show that clarity of explanation never matters. Here, the content was the same in both versions.'
  ],
  links: [['active-learning-feels-worse', 'citedby', 'What happens when this idea moves from a video to a real classroom.'], ['fonts-fortune', 'cites', 'From the other side: what if making things less fluent helped?'], ['koriat-cues', 'idea', 'The framework for why it happens: judgments of learning are built from cues.']] },

{ id: 'ai-without-guardrails', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'bastani2025', design: 'field experiment (randomised)', isNew: false,
  head: 'chatgpt raised practice scores by 48%, then lowered exam scores by 17%',
  card: 'Hamsa Bastani and colleagues gave nearly 1,000 high school maths students in Turkey access to a standard GPT-4 interface, a version with learning safeguards, or nothing. During practice, the AI groups did better, 48% and 127%. On an exam with no help, students who’d had standard ChatGPT-style access did 17% worse than those who’d had nothing. The tutor version erased that harm, without giving a benefit. Students didn’t seem to notice.',
  story: [
    'The concern here is one that pilots know well: a tool that performs for you can quietly erode your ability when it’s gone. The authors explicitly invoke autopilot [[bastani2025|introduction]].',
    'In four 90-minute sessions across about fifty classes, roughly 15% of a semester’s maths, students practised with course notes plus either GPT Base (mimicking ChatGPT), GPT Tutor (with teacher-informed guardrails and correct solutions built in), or no AI. Then they took an exam alone, and it counted toward grades [[bastani2025|Experimental Design]].',
    'During practice, the Tutor group did 127% better than controls, and the Base group 48% better. On the exam, the Base group did 17% worse than controls. The Tutor version essentially eliminated the harm, but with no positive effect either [[bastani2025|introduction]]. Students often used GPT Base as a crutch by asking for and copying answers, and they didn’t perceive any loss [[bastani2025|introduction]].',
    'The limits matter. This was at one school in Turkey, measuring short-term outcomes only, as the authors point out [[bastani2025|Experimental Design]]. It’s specifically about access during practice, then removal. A published correction only fixes one author’s affiliation [[bastani2025|Crossref update record]].'
  ],
  links: [['ai-tutor-harvard', 'cousin', 'A complicating result published the same year: a carefully designed AI tutor beat in-class active learning.'], ['active-learning-feels-worse', 'cites', 'It cites this paper. The same theme: people’s sense of their learning isn’t a good guide.'], ['ai-petitions', 'cousin', 'A very different question about what chatting with AI does to people.']] },

{ id: 'ai-tutor-harvard', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'kestin2025', design: 'randomised crossover classroom experiment',
  head: 'an ai tutor beat a good active-learning class, in one harvard physics course',
  card: 'Greg Kestin and colleagues had 194 Harvard physics students alternate between an in-class active-learning lesson and a custom AI tutor at home. Median post-test scores were 4.5 with the tutor and 3.5 in class, from a baseline of 2.75, and the median time on the tutor was 49 minutes, versus 60 in class. Students felt more engaged too. But the tutor was carefully engineered, the test immediate, and the setting unusual.',
  story: [
    'This study is unusually useful because the comparison is strong. The control is not a lecture, but a class built on best practices, and the AI tutor was purpose-built along the same principles and walked students through each part of each problem [[kestin2025|Results; Methods]].',
    'Group 1 used the tutor at home in the first week, while Group 2 was in class; then they swapped, with surface tension and fluid flow as topics [[kestin2025|Results]]. Median post-test scores were 4.5 (AI, N = 142) versus 3.5 (in class, N = 174), against a pre-test baseline of 2.75, so gains were over double (<em>z</em> = −5.6, <em>p</em> &lt; 10<sup>−8</sup>) [[kestin2025|Results]]. Seventy percent of AI-group students spent under 60 minutes, and time on task did not correlate with post-test scores [[kestin2025|Time on task]].',
    'Their conclusions are confident, and readers should supply some brakes. These are two lessons in one elite university course with a custom tool, measured right after each lesson. Set this beside the high-school study of generic ChatGPT, which found the opposite on a later unaided test: the two are not contradictory so much as different designs, tools, students and outcomes. The claim here rests on tests taken right after each lesson, not on later retention.'
  ],
  links: [['ai-without-guardrails', 'cousin', 'A different tool, population and measure, and a much less rosy outcome.'], ['active-learning-feels-worse', 'cites', 'Overlapping authors. The earlier paper on what active classes do and how they feel.']] },

{ id: 'growth-mindset-national', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'yeager2019', design: 'preregistered national randomised experiment',
  head: 'an hour-long mindset course nudged grades, but only for some students, in some schools',
  card: 'Most interventions shrink at scale, and mindset research has had its fights. David Yeager and colleagues ran a short online growth-mindset course with 12,490 ninth graders in a nationally representative sample of 65 US schools. Lower-achieving students’ core grades rose about 0.10 points; higher achievers’ didn’t change. The effect depended on context: it was larger where peer norms supported taking on challenges. A modest effect, but a careful one.',
  story: [
    'The appeal of mindset interventions is obvious: under an hour, online, cheap. The paper’s best contribution, though, is its focus on heterogeneity, on where an effect does and doesn’t appear, because average effects hide a lot [[yeager2019|A focus on heterogeneity]].',
    'The sample was a stratified random draw of 65 regular US public schools, with 12,490 ninth graders individually randomised. Data were collected independently, analyses preregistered, and results corroborated by a blinded Bayesian analysis [[yeager2019|Methods; abstract]].',
    'Among the targeted 6,320 lower-achieving students, core course GPA rose by 0.10 grade points. Among higher-achieving students, it rose by 0.01, which was not significant [[yeager2019|Results]]. The intervention also increased overall enrolment in advanced maths [[yeager2019|abstract]]. Peer norms mattered, too: the effect on GPA was greater where the school’s peers were more willing to take on challenges (<em>B</em> = 0.11, <em>p</em> = .029) [[yeager2019|Moderation by peer norms]].',
    'For context, two meta-analyses by Sisk and colleagues found weak overall effects of mind-sets and mindset interventions, with some support for the benefit to academically at-risk or low-SES students [[sisk2018|abstract]]. So this is not the magic bullet of early headlines, nor is it nothing.'
  ],
  numbers: ['Higher-achieving students: <em>B</em> = 0.01, 95% CI [−0.03, 0.06], <em>n</em> = 6,170, <em>k</em> = 65, <em>p</em> = .634. Norms × treatment for lower-achieving students: <em>B</em> = 0.11, 95% CI [0.01, 0.21], <em>n</em> = 6,320, <em>k</em> = 65, β = 0.23 [[yeager2019|Results]].'],
  links: [['mindset-meta-analysis', 'cousin', 'The skeptical big picture. It isn’t a direct reply, but you should read them together.'], ['stereotype-threat-rrr', 'cousin', 'Another social-psychological intervention idea, under much harsher replication conditions.'], ['marshmallow-follow-up', 'cousin', 'Another popular idea about character and success, viewed at a much larger scale.']] },

{ id: 'mindset-meta-analysis', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'sisk2018', design: 'two meta-analyses',
  head: 'how much does mindset matter? two meta-analyses say: a little, for some',
  card: 'Victoria Sisk and colleagues ran two meta-analyses, one on how mindset relates to achievement (k = 273, N = 365,915), and one on whether mindset interventions help (k = 43, N = 57,155). They report that overall effects were weak in both. But some results did support specific parts of the theory: students with low socioeconomic status or at academic risk might benefit from these interventions. It reads like a useful cold shower.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Sisk, Burgoyne, Sun, Butler and Macnamara report that overall effects were weak for both meta-analyses (mind-set and achievement, k = 273, N = 365,915; interventions, k = 43, N = 57,155), though some results supported specific tenets of the theory, namely that students with low socioeconomic status or who are academically at risk might benefit from interventions [[sisk2018|abstract]].'],
  links: [['growth-mindset-national', 'cousin', 'The largest later test of the idea that it might help some students, in certain settings.'], ['learning-styles-kids', 'cousin', 'Another popular classroom idea held up to scrutiny.']] },

{ id: 'marshmallow-trust', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'kidd2013', design: 'randomised experiment (N = 28)',
  head: 'children waited for the second marshmallow only when adults kept their promises',
  card: 'Is the marshmallow test about self-control? Celeste Kidd and colleagues first showed 28 children either a reliable adult, who returned with promised art supplies, or an unreliable one, who didn’t. Then came the marshmallow. Children in the reliable condition waited a mean of 12 minutes; those in the unreliable condition, 3. So waiting may reflect a reasonable bet about whether the world delivers. It’s a small study with a large and clean effect.',
  story: [
    'If you’ve just been told “wait and I’ll bring something better” and it did not come, eating the marshmallow now isn’t weakness. It’s good inference. That is the alternative to the “deficient capacity” story [[kidd2013|1.2]].',
    'Twenty-eight children aged about three and a half to nearly six did an art project with the researcher, who promised better crayons, then better stickers, and in one condition delivered, and in the other didn’t. Children were randomly assigned, with groups balanced for gender and age [[kidd2013|2.1–2.2]]. Next came the standard task, with a covert webcam and blind coders timing the first lick or bite [[kidd2013|2.2.2–2.2.3]].',
    'Mean wait was 3 minutes 2 seconds in the unreliable condition and 12 minutes 2 seconds in the reliable one (<em>p</em> &lt; .0005). Because the task stopped at 15 minutes, that is likely conservative [[kidd2013|Results]].',
    'This is a tiny experiment, 14 children per group, and it was not designed to explain the long-term correlations for which the marshmallow test became famous. The authors say that wait times may reflect beliefs about the world in addition to self-control, not instead of it [[kidd2013|abstract]].'
  ],
  links: [['marshmallow-follow-up', 'citedby', 'A far larger study, which cites this one, asks what early waiting really predicts.'], ['hungry-judges-challenge', 'idea', 'A similar twist: what looks like a mental lapse might be the situation.'], ['overimitation', 'cousin', 'Another case where children’s apparently strange behaviour may be sensible inference.']] },

{ id: 'marshmallow-follow-up', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'watts2018', design: 'longitudinal correlational study',
  head: 'the marshmallow test predicts far less than its reputation',
  card: 'Tyler Watts, Greg Duncan and Haonan Quan revisited the famous link, in a sample of 918 children, focusing on the 552 whose mothers hadn’t finished college. Each extra minute waited at age 4 predicted about a tenth of a standard deviation in achievement at 15. But that was half the original size, and shrank by two thirds after accounting for family background, early ability and home environment. Most of the variation came from waiting just 20 seconds.',
  story: [
    'Original follow-ups came from small samples of children at a Stanford preschool: correlations as high as .57 with SAT scores rested on samples as small as 35, with no adjustment for family background [[watts2018|introduction]]. So Watts and colleagues used a much larger national dataset, with delay measured at 54 months and outcomes at age 15 (n = 918) [[watts2018|Current Study]].',
    'They concentrated on children whose mothers had not completed college (n = 552), a sample ten times the size of the 1990 study’s [[watts2018|Current Study]]. An extra minute predicted about a tenth of a standard deviation gain in achievement at 15. That association was half the original size, and dropped by two-thirds with controls [[watts2018|abstract]]. And most of the variation came from being able to wait at least 20 seconds [[watts2018|abstract]]. Links to behavioural outcomes were much smaller and rarely significant [[watts2018|abstract]].',
    'Note what this is. It’s a conceptual replication with a different delay limit (7 minutes), and it’s correlational, so neither version of the finding shows that teaching children to wait would improve their lives. There’s also a helpful point here about strong interpretations: if associations come from factors like family environment, then boosting waiting alone might do little [[watts2018|introduction]].'
  ],
  numbers: ['In the lower-SES sample, 45% of children waited the maximum 7 minutes and 23% waited less than 20 s. In the higher-SES sample, only 10% waited less than 20 s, and the mean was 5.38 minutes [[watts2018|Results]]. Children who waited between 20 s and 2 min scored about a third of a standard deviation higher than those who waited under 20 s [[watts2018|Results]].'],
  links: [['marshmallow-trust', 'cites', 'An experiment suggesting waiting partly reflects how much the world can be trusted.'], ['growth-mindset-national', 'cousin', 'Another big-sample look at a beloved story about character and school.'], ['ego-depletion-many-labs', 'cousin', 'Self-control in the lab, on trial.']] },

{ id: 'sleepmore-seattle', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'dunster2018', design: 'pre/post natural experiment',
  head: 'when seattle started school later, teenagers actually slept more',
  card: 'Would teens just stay up later if school started later? When Seattle delayed secondary start times by nearly an hour, Gideon Dunster and colleagues put activity monitors on sophomores before and after. Median school-night sleep rose by 34 minutes, because wake-up moved later while bedtime largely didn’t. Median grades went from 77.5% to 82%. It isn’t randomised, and the authors are cautious about crediting sleep for the grades, but it is objective.',
  story: [
    'Earlier work relied mainly on surveys. This study used wrist activity monitors, as part of a science class, with different students taking the same class at two public high schools in the year before and the year after the change [[dunster2018|Results]].',
    'After the change, median wake-up was 44 minutes later, and sleep onset was only non-significantly later, so median sleep duration on school days rose 34 minutes (<em>p</em> = .0007). Non-school days didn’t change, which is what you’d expect if the school bell was the cause [[dunster2018|Results]]. Social jet lag also shrank [[dunster2018|Results]].',
    'Median grades rose from 77.5% to 82%, and sleepiness fell from 7.0 to 6.0. Attendance was more mixed: Franklin High saw fewer tardies and absences, but Roosevelt saw no change [[dunster2018|Results]].',
    'The authors are appropriately careful: reduced sleepiness is plausibly due to more sleep, but it’s much harder to attribute the grade increase to it [[dunster2018|Discussion]]. Students in the two years were different individuals, and there was no comparison district. Still, the practical answer to the “they’ll just stay up later” objection looks like: not by much.'
  ],
  how: ['Pre/post with independent samples of sophomores in the same science class at two schools, in the same part of each year. Social jet lag: median 1.25 (n = 76) in 2017 vs 1.60 (n = 81) in 2016, <em>p</em> = .0118 [[dunster2018|Results]].'],
  links: [['ebbinghaus-again', 'cousin', 'A bump in the forgetting curve at 24 hours, which some have linked to sleep.'], ['classroom-decorations', 'cousin', 'Another simple change to the setting of school.'], ['covid-first-graders', 'cousin', 'Another natural experiment in how school is organised, with a surprise.']] },

{ id: 'learning-styles-kids', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'rogowsky2020', design: 'school-based experiment with crossed instruction modes',
  head: 'fifth graders who call themselves visual learners didn’t learn better by seeing',
  card: 'Is matching teaching to a student’s preferred style useful? Beth Rogowsky and colleagues tested 125 fifth graders with a learning-styles inventory and gave them passages by reading and by listening. Style preference showed no relationship with comprehension in the matched format, immediately or two weeks later. This fits earlier adult findings, and the demanding test proposed by Pashler and colleagues. It covers only the visual/auditory distinction, as the authors make clear.',
  story: [
    'The idea of the “visual learner” is everywhere, but a demanding review argued few studies have even used a design capable of testing it. You need learners assessed the same way, then assigned different instruction, with the same test across groups, looking for a specific interaction [[rogowsky2020|abstract; introduction]].',
    'This follows that design: an entire fifth grade from a rural Pennsylvania public middle school (n = 125), with a learning-styles inventory built for ages 10–13 and comprehension tested by both reading and listening. The school had no learning-styles curriculum; a power analysis gave a minimum of 76 students [[rogowsky2020|Participants]].',
    'Contrary to the hypothesis, there was no significant positive relationship between auditory preference and listening comprehension, or between visual preference and reading comprehension, and no relationship between style, instruction mode and performance immediately or at two weeks [[rogowsky2020|Results]].',
    'The authors emphasise limits: the results can’t be generalised to other learning styles, instruction, durations or materials [[rogowsky2020|Results]]. Preferences themselves are real. It’s just that here they don’t help predict what teaching works.'
  ],
  links: [['music-training-meta', 'cousin', 'Another classroom favourite under strict controls.'], ['feedback-revisited', 'cousin', 'And one classroom idea that holds up, with lots of asterisks.'], ['mindset-meta-analysis', 'cousin', 'Another popular theory of learners, weighed at scale.']] },

{ id: 'music-training-meta', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'sala2020', design: 'multilevel meta-analysis',
  head: 'music lessons don’t appear to boost children’s other skills, in the better experiments',
  card: 'It’s a beloved idea that music lessons make kids smarter. Giovanni Sala and Fernand Gobet’s meta-analysis found a small overall effect (g = 0.184). But the design matters a lot. Studies using active control groups or random assignment showed effects around zero. The apparent benefit shrinks as study quality rises. None of this says music isn’t worthwhile. It suggests you probably shouldn’t expect it to raise maths scores along the way.',
  story: [
    'Musicians do tend to read better, and to do better in school, so why not? Because correlation leaves open who ends up taking music lessons. Experiments are how you’d check [[sala2020|introduction]].',
    'Sala and Gobet pooled experimental studies, including the two largest randomised trials in this field, with explicit modelling of design quality [[sala2020|The present meta-analysis]].',
    'The overall effect on cognitive and academic outcomes was weak and moderately heterogeneous (<em>g</em> = 0.184, <em>SE</em> = 0.041). The informative part is its pattern: there’s an inverse relationship between design quality and effect size. Studies with active controls or random assignment show homogeneous null or near-zero effects [[sala2020|Discussion]].',
    'It’s only about music training’s side effects on other skills, and only in children. It doesn’t speak to what music does for musical ability or for enjoyment. Which, to be fair, is why most people pick up an instrument.'
  ],
  links: [['learning-styles-kids', 'cousin', 'Another appealing idea, tested with the design it deserves.'], ['feedback-revisited', 'cousin', 'Meta-analysis again, on a technique with far more going for it.']] },

{ id: 'feedback-revisited', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'wisniewski2020', design: 'meta-analysis',
  head: 'feedback helps, but less simply than the famous number suggests',
  card: 'John Hattie’s synthesis made feedback one of education’s superstar interventions, with d = 0.79. Benedikt Wisniewski and colleagues redid the analysis at the level of primary studies, and got d = 0.48: still respectable, but far from the headline. The bigger message is its variability, and earlier work had noted that about a third of feedback effects can be negative. “Give feedback” isn’t quite advice until you know which kind, about what.',
  story: [
    'Feedback is close to a sacred cow in education, with much of that reputation resting on syntheses that averaged earlier meta-analyses and gave each one equal weight, regardless of how many studies it held [[wisniewski2020|introduction; Discussion]].',
    'Wisniewski, Zierer and Hattie (yes, Hattie is a coauthor, which makes this rather more interesting) revisited the question by integrating primary studies directly. They got an average effect of <em>d</em> = 0.48, medium to high, against <em>d</em> = 0.79 in the earlier synthesis. They emphasise that “the variability of the effects is most notable” [[wisniewski2020|Discussion]].',
    'That variability echoes an older result. Kluger and DeNisi’s 1996 review of 131 studies, as summarised here, had an average effect of 0.38 but found that about a third of effects were negative [[wisniewski2020|introduction]]. So feedback isn’t safely good; sometimes it’s a net harm.'
  ],
  links: [['music-training-meta', 'cousin', 'Another meta-analysis where the design of the included studies changes the answer.'], ['active-learning-feels-worse', 'cousin', 'And another argument about what really helps in a classroom.']] },

{ id: 'covid-first-graders', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'covid2026', design: 'multicohort study', isNew: true,
  head: 'first graders in one german state did not fall behind in lockdown, on average',
  card: 'Most studies of school closures found losses. But Jessika Golle and colleagues, looking at 2,740 first graders in Baden-Württemberg, report no overall decline in achievement under pandemic schooling. High teacher–student contact, in fact, was associated with higher maths achievement than regular schooling. Motivation told a different story, with declines in interest and self-concept in language, especially with little contact and low family socioeconomic status. These are associations, in one region, at one age.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Golle and colleagues analysed multicohort data collected at the start and end of the school year from 2,740 first graders in Baden-Württemberg. Against expectations, they report no overall decline in achievement, higher maths achievement associated with high teacher–student contact compared with regular schooling, and negative average effects on interest and self-concept in language, particularly with low contact and low family socioeconomic status [[covid2026|abstract]].'],
  links: [['sleepmore-seattle', 'cousin', 'Another natural experiment on how school is organised.'], ['classroom-decorations', 'cousin', 'And a controlled one, on what a classroom looks like.']] },

{ id: 'classroom-decorations', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'fisher2014', design: 'lab classroom experiment',
  head: 'kindergarteners learned less when the walls were covered with decorations',
  card: 'Anna Fisher, Karrie Godwin and Howard Seltman built a laboratory classroom for six science lessons and varied how decorated the walls were. They report that with highly decorated walls, young children were more distracted by the visual environment, spent more time off task, and showed smaller learning gains than when the decorations were removed. It’s one small setting, but a question every teacher with a staple gun has probably wondered about.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Fisher, Godwin and Seltman placed kindergarten children in a laboratory classroom for six introductory science lessons and experimentally manipulated its visual environment. They report that children were more distracted, spent more time off task and showed smaller learning gains when the walls were highly decorated than when decorations were removed [[fisher2014|abstract]].'],
  links: [['invisible-gorilla-original', 'cousin', 'From five-year-olds to adults: the limits of attention, in the classic demonstration.'], ['sleepmore-seattle', 'cousin', 'Another change to the setting of school, and what it did.']] },

{ id: 'testing-effect', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'roediger2006', design: 'lab experiments', classic: true,
  head: 'cramming wins at five minutes. testing wins at a week',
  card: 'Henry Roediger and Jeffrey Karpicke had students study prose passages and then either restudy them or take free-recall tests with no feedback. After five minutes, restudying gave better recall. On delayed tests, two days or a week later, testing gave substantially better retention. And here’s the best part: repeated studying raised students’ confidence about how well they’d remember. The strategy that felt best was not the one that lasted.',
  story: ['From the abstract: students studied passages and took one or three immediate free-recall tests, without feedback, or restudied. After 5 minutes, repeated studying improved recall relative to repeated testing. On delayed tests, prior testing produced substantially greater retention, even though repeated studying increased students’ confidence in their ability to remember [[roediger2006|abstract]].'],
  links: [['interleaving-rct', 'cousin', 'Another study technique with a real classroom trial behind it.'], ['active-learning-feels-worse', 'idea', 'The same disconnect, in a classroom, between how learning feels and what it does.'], ['method-of-loci', 'cousin', 'A far more elaborate way of making memories durable.']] },

{ id: 'interleaving-rct', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'rohrer2020', design: 'preregistered cluster RCT',
  head: 'mixing up maths problems raised test scores, from 38% to 61%',
  card: 'Most maths homework practises one skill at a time. Doug Rohrer and colleagues ran a preregistered randomised trial: 54 seventh-grade classes did interleaved or blocked assignments for 4 months, then took an unannounced test a month later. They report that the interleaved group outscored the blocked group, 61% to 38% (d = 0.83). Teachers implemented it without training. The authors note important caveats remain. It’s a big effect, even so.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Rohrer, Dedrick, Hartwig and Cheung report a preregistered cluster randomised trial in which 54 7th-grade mathematics classes periodically completed interleaved or blocked assignments over 4 months, followed by an interleaved review assignment. One month later, on an unannounced test, the interleaved group outscored the blocked group, 61% vs 38%, d = 0.83. Teachers implemented the intervention without training and later expressed support in an anonymous survey, though the authors say important caveats remain [[rohrer2020|abstract]].'],
  links: [['effort-feels-like-failing', 'idea', 'Why students avoid exactly this strategy, even when it works.'], ['testing-effect', 'cousin', 'Another study practice that beats its reputation.'], ['productive-failure', 'cousin', 'One more case for doing the harder thing first.']] },

{ id: 'productive-failure', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'sinha2021', design: 'meta-analysis',
  head: 'struggling with a problem before instruction may work better than the reverse',
  card: 'Should you explain first, or let students struggle first? Tanmay Sinha and Manu Kapur’s meta-analysis of 53 studies and 166 comparisons reports a moderate effect favouring problem solving before instruction (g = 0.36), and stronger effects when it follows “productive failure” principles. Younger learners and domain-general skills, however, favoured instruction first. And there’s an authors’ estimate that is worth reading sceptically: a much higher effect after accounting for publication bias.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Sinha and Kapur report a significant moderate effect in favour of problem solving followed by instruction (Hedges’ g 0.36, 95% CI [0.20, 0.51]) across 53 studies and 166 comparisons, stronger (0.37–0.58) with high fidelity to productive failure. Contrasting trends favoured instruction first for second to fifth graders and for domain-general skills. They also give an estimated g of 0.87 after accounting for publication bias [[sinha2021|abstract]]. Publication-bias corrections usually shrink effects, so that last figure deserves a raised eyebrow; that remark is mine.'],
  links: [['active-learning-feels-worse', 'idea', 'Struggle, seen from the students’ perspective.'], ['effort-feels-like-failing', 'idea', 'What students make of that struggle.']] },

{ id: 'fonts-fortune', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'diemand2011', design: 'lab and classroom studies',
  head: 'harder-to-read fonts improved memory, said one much-noticed paper',
  card: 'Connor Diemand-Yauman, Daniel Oppenheimer and Erikka Vaughan reported that information in hard-to-read fonts was remembered better, both in the lab and in high school classrooms. Their suggestion: superficial changes to materials could yield significant improvements in education. It was exactly the kind of finding that everyone wants to pass along, with obvious appeal to anyone designing a worksheet. Now look at what happened in the attempts that followed.',
  story: ['From the abstract: the authors report that information in hard-to-read fonts was better remembered than easier-to-read information in a controlled setting (Study 1), and extend this to high school classrooms (Study 2), suggesting that superficial changes to learning materials could yield significant improvements in educational outcomes [[diemand2011|abstract]].'],
  links: [['disfluent-maths', 'citedby', 'A pooled analysis of a related font claim, finding no effect on maths reasoning.'], ['sans-forgetica', 'citedby', 'A font designed on this logic, tested in four experiments.'], ['fluent-lecturer', 'citedby', 'The other side of fluency: what polish does to how much you think you learned.']] },

{ id: 'disfluent-maths', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'meyer2015', design: 'pooled replications', note: 'failed replication',
  head: 'seventeen data sets: a blurry font doesn’t make you better at trick maths problems',
  card: 'A famous demonstration suggested people solve counterintuitive maths problems better when printed in a hard-to-read font. Andrew Meyer, Shane Frederick and colleagues pooled that experiment with 16 attempts to replicate it, and report no effect on solution rates. Cognitive ability, format and setting didn’t rescue it either. Disfluent fonts slightly slow people down, they add, but there’s little evidence that this switches on analytic reasoning.',
  story: ['From the abstract: after pooling the original experiment with 16 replication attempts, Meyer and colleagues find no effect on solution rates and no evidence of a benefit under any of the examined moderators, including cognitive ability, presentation format and experimental setting. Disfluent fonts slightly increase response times, but there is little evidence that they activate analytic reasoning [[meyer2015|abstract]].'],
  links: [['fonts-fortune', 'cites', 'An earlier paper about the educational promise of disfluent fonts.'], ['sans-forgetica', 'cousin', 'The same doubts, about a font created for the purpose.']] },

{ id: 'sans-forgetica', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'taylor2020', design: 'four experiments', note: 'null result',
  head: 'a font built to help you remember did not help you remember',
  card: 'Sans Forgetica was designed with gaps in its letters, on the theory that reading difficulty aids learning. Andrea Taylor and colleagues ran four experiments trying to replicate its creators’ unpublished findings. People did rate it as harder to read than Arial, so the font does what it says. But memory was equivalent, and sometimes even worse. Difficulty is not automatically “desirable difficulty”. A pity, because it’s a lovely idea.',
  story: ['From the abstract: across four experiments, Taylor and colleagues had subjects read information in Sans Forgetica or Arial and rate its difficulty or attempt to recall it. Subjects rated it harder to read, but it led to equivalent memory performance and sometimes impaired it [[taylor2020|abstract]].'],
  links: [['fonts-fortune', 'cites', 'Where the reasoning came from.'], ['bionic-reading', 'cousin', 'A 2026 test of another typographic hack.'], ['laptop-notes', 'cites', 'Also in its reference list: the study arguing that longhand works because it forces deeper processing.']] },

{ id: 'laptop-notes', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'mueller2014', design: 'three experiments', classic: false,
  head: 'the pen beats the keyboard, says the study everyone has heard about',
  card: 'Pam Mueller and Daniel Oppenheimer reported in three studies that students taking laptop notes did worse on conceptual questions than those writing longhand, even with no distractions. Their explanation: laptop notes tend toward verbatim transcription rather than reframing in your own words. It became the reason many professors banned laptops. A published replication has since complicated things, so be sure to follow the link before quoting this one.',
  story: ['From the abstract: in three studies, students who took notes on laptops performed worse on conceptual questions than those taking notes longhand. The authors report that while more notes can help, laptop users’ tendency to transcribe verbatim is detrimental [[mueller2014|abstract]]. A 2018 corrigendum fixes errors in how the statistics were calculated and reported. The authors say the results remain qualitatively the same [[mueller2014|Crossref update record]].'],
  links: [['laptop-notes-replication', 'failedby', 'A direct replication, plus mini meta-analyses of similar studies.'], ['fonts-fortune', 'cites', 'Same co-author, same broad bet on the benefits of working harder.']] },

{ id: 'laptop-notes-replication', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'urry2021', design: 'direct replication with mini meta-analyses', note: 'failed replication',
  head: 'don’t ditch the laptop just yet',
  card: 'Heather Urry and colleagues repeated Mueller and Oppenheimer’s Study 1 (laptop n = 74, longhand n = 68). As in the original, laptop users took more notes and copied more verbatim. But all those notes didn’t earn them better quiz scores, and mini meta-analyses of eight similar studies echoed this. More notes went with better quiz scores, though the verbatim penalty wasn’t robust. Longhand, the authors conclude, doesn’t appear to improve immediate learning through better encoding.',
  story: ['From the abstract: in a direct replication with laptop (n = 74) and longhand (n = 68) conditions, laptop participants took notes with more verbatim overlap and more words, but did not perform better on the quiz, and exploratory meta-analyses of eight similar studies echoed this. Higher word count was associated with better quiz performance; the association of verbatim overlap with worse performance was not robust in the replication. The authors say the results do not support the idea that longhand improves immediate learning via better encoding [[urry2021|abstract]].'],
  links: [['laptop-notes', 'failed', 'The 2014 original this replication re-ran.'], ['disfluent-maths', 'cousin', 'A similar story: a tidy cognitive explanation, then a more sobering pooled analysis.']] },

{ id: 'overimitation', cat: 'educational', level: 'abstract', bib: 'lyons2007', design: 'experiments with young children', classic: true,
  head: 'children copy pointless actions because they think those actions matter',
  card: 'Show a young child how to operate a new object, including some unnecessary steps, and they’ll tend to repeat those steps, even ones chimpanzees ignore. It was long seen as mainly social. Derek Lyons, Andrew Young and Frank Keil report that children who watch an adult intentionally manipulate an object encode all the actions as causally meaningful. It’s a fast way to learn opaque things, and it has a price.',
  story: ['From the abstract: Lyons, Young and Keil report that children who observe an adult intentionally manipulating a novel object have a strong tendency to encode all of the adult’s actions as causally meaningful, implicitly revising their causal understanding of the object. They suggest this automatic encoding lets children rapidly calibrate causal beliefs about opaque systems but also carries a cost when some of the adult’s purposeful actions are unnecessary [[lyons2007|abstract]].'],
  links: [['infants-helpers', 'cousin', 'Another account of what very young minds take from watching others.'], ['marshmallow-trust', 'cousin', 'Another case where children’s behaviour looks more rational, once you think from their side.']] },

{ id: 'pictures-mislead', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'jaeger2023', digest: 'cues', design: 'four between-subjects experiments',
  head: 'pictures in a textbook can make you worse at knowing what you understand',
  card: 'Adding images to a science text left comprehension unchanged in Allison Jaeger and Logan Fiorella’s first study, yet it weakened how well students could judge their own understanding of the material. The catch: which judgment suffered kept moving between studies. It affected draw judgments in the first and test judgments in the last. They propose images may lend a false sense of fluency. It’s a neat idea, and one these studies didn’t directly test.',
  story: [
    'Instructional visuals are supposed to help. Jaeger and Fiorella asked a further question: do they help students <em>know</em> how well they understand? They were especially curious about judging your ability to draw the material, since students might read a provided picture as evidence that they could draw it [[jaeger2023|Types of JOLs; The Present Studies]].',
    'Undergraduates read four expository texts, with or without images designed to be conceptual and redundant, gave judgments of how they would do, and took tests. Comprehension didn’t change with images (Study 1a: <em>d</em> = 0.01) [[jaeger2023|Study 1a]]. But the relation between people’s judgments and their performance across texts weakened for draw judgments (<em>d</em> = 0.51) and, in the larger final study, for test judgments (η<sub>p</sub>² = .05), not draw judgments [[jaeger2023|Studies 1a, 3]].',
    'That inconsistency is the honest core of this paper. The authors themselves say which judgment showed the harm varied across studies, and that the draw effect did not replicate in the final study [[jaeger2023|General Discussion]]. Their proposal, that images produce a misattributed sense of fluency, goes beyond what the experiments isolate. As a reader of many cheerful infographics, I find it a nice thing to be slightly suspicious about.'
  ],
  links: [['koriat-cues', 'cites', 'The cue-utilization framework this paper explicitly uses to make sense of bad self-assessment.'], ['fluent-lecturer', 'cousin', 'A different kind of polish, a similar illusion.']] },

{ id: 'effort-feels-like-failing', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'onan2022', digest: 'effort', design: 'preregistered mixed-methods experiment',
  head: 'interleaving feels like failing, until, later in the session, it doesn’t',
  card: 'In Erdem Onan and colleagues’ preregistered study of 150 students, mixing up artists in practice beat studying them one at a time (d = 0.76). It felt worse at first. But over the session, effort fell and perceived learning rose, and those shifts predicted later choosing it: from 13% by habit to 40%. Showing students a graph of their own experiences, however, made no reliable difference. Experience, it seems, was the better persuader.',
  story: [
    'Students tend to avoid interleaved practice, despite how well it usually works. The misinterpreted-effort hypothesis says that’s because effort feels like a sign you aren’t learning. Onan and colleagues asked how this experience changes as you continue [[onan2022|The Present Study]].',
    'Participants (N = 150) studied paintings by 12 artists, blocked or interleaved, rating effort and learning after each unit. Later they chose a strategy for new artists, after a visual prompt of their own ratings or a control task [[onan2022|Method]].',
    'Interleaving won on classification (5.95 vs 3.11; <em>d</em> = 0.76). Effort was higher for interleaving and perceived learning higher for blocking, but over time, effort fell and perceived learning rose for interleaving only. Choice of interleaving went from 13% to 34% to 40%, with the prompt adding nothing reliable (<em>p</em> = .142) [[onan2022|Results]]. Rising perceived learning statistically accounted for the link between falling effort and choosing interleaving, though that ordering is modelled, not manipulated [[onan2022|Strategy Decisions Across Time]].',
    'Students who chose blocking came to think worse of interleaving, while those who chose interleaving reversed their initial preference: the same session, with diverging beliefs [[onan2022|Learning Strategy Belief Changes]]. All this happened online, in a single session, with no stakes [[onan2022|Limitations]].'
  ],
  links: [['interleaving-rct', 'cousin', 'The same technique in 54 real classrooms.'], ['active-learning-feels-worse', 'idea', 'The same disconnect, shown in a physics classroom.'], ['surname-frequency', 'idea', 'A conceptual parallel: a judgment turns on how you interpret a mental experience.']] },

{ id: 'judgments-of-importance', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'murphy2022', digest: 'control', design: 'within- and between-subjects experiments',
  head: 'asking how important something is changes what people choose to study',
  card: 'Imagine babysitting 24 children, each with foods they like, dislike and are allergic to. Dillon Murphy, Kara Hoover and Alan Castel found people studied in screen order and did best on likes, not allergies. But asking them to judge how important each category was shifted study time toward allergies and, with whole-category judgments, improved allergy memory. Asking people to predict how likely they were to remember something did far less.',
  story: [
    '“Responsible remembering” is the idea that people prioritise what would be costly to forget. This paper asks how, specifically through the allocation of self-paced study time [[murphy2022|introduction]].',
    'Without any judgments, participants spent the most time on likes (7.01 s, vs about 5.5 s for others) and remembered those best (.34 vs .17 and .17), which was the category listed first on screen [[murphy2022|Exp. 1a]]. So left to themselves, people followed the page. That confound with position is worth keeping in mind, since within this paper position and category cannot be separated [[murphy2022|Exp. 1a, Discussion]].',
    'Judgments of importance redirected this: people studied allergies longer and recalled them better than those making judgments of learning (<em>p</em><sub>holm</sub> = .001), with allergy study time increasing across lists [[murphy2022|Exp. 2a]]. With item-level judgments, study time shifted again, but allergy recall no better than the comparison group’s (<em>p</em><sub>holm</sub> &gt; .999) [[murphy2022|Exp. 2b]]. So it’s the study choices that were most reliably moved.',
    'The stakes here are imagined, with young adults online or in a lab, so whether it generalises to real caregiving remains open [[murphy2022|General discussion]]. The journal’s later correction only adds a funding note [[murphy2022|Crossref update record]].'
  ],
  links: [['judgments-drive-study', 'cites', 'The earlier causal test that monitoring judgments shape study choice, which this paper cites.'], ['koriat-cues', 'cites', 'The cue framework this paper uses to think about what people relied on instead.']] },

{ id: 'judgments-drive-study', cat: 'educational', level: 'checked', bib: 'metcalfefinn2008', design: 'three experiments', classic: true,
  head: 'what you think you know decides what you restudy, even when you’re wrong',
  card: 'Do judgments of learning actually drive study choices, or just come along for the ride? Janet Metcalfe and Bridgid Finn tricked them apart. Pairs studied once then three times, or three times then once, were recalled equally well (.72 and .69), but people thought the first kind was weaker, and chose to restudy more of those (.51 vs .38). When delayed judgments removed that illusion, the choice difference vanished as well.',
  story: [
    'The elegant part is dissociating judgment from memory. In Experiment 1 (N = 22), items studied 1 then 3 times, or 3 then 1 times, had the same recall on the next trial (.72 vs .69), but immediate judgments were lower for 1–3 items (.70 vs .78), and people chose to restudy more of them (.51 vs .38, <em>t</em>(21) = 4.33, <em>p</em> &lt; .001) [[metcalfefinn2008|Exp. 1, p. 175]].',
    'Experiment 2 (N = 50) used delayed judgments, which removed the judgment difference (.60 vs .61) and the choice difference with it (.43 vs .44); immediate judgments reproduced both [[metcalfefinn2008|Exp. 2, p. 177]]. Experiment 3 (N = 24) did it another way, with forward and backward pairs: recall differed sharply, but judgments and choices did not [[metcalfefinn2008|Exp. 3, p. 178]].',
    'In short: when memory and judgment disagree, choice follows the judgment. That makes your feeling of knowing more than a bystander, and it’s why an inaccurate feeling can be costly.'
  ],
  links: [['judgments-of-importance', 'citedby', 'A later paper, citing this one: change the judgment, and you change what gets studied.'], ['koriat-cues', 'idea', 'Where those judgments come from.']] }
];
